QB.add(37,[{"id":37001,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"A student compares an antibody with a cytokine. Which statement about their sizes is correct?","o":["Antibodies are about 150 kDa, much larger than cytokines at about 30 kDa","Antibodies are about 30 kDa, smaller than cytokines at about 150 kDa","Both are about 150 kDa, because both must be able to move freely between neighbouring cells","Antibodies are about 50 kDa, the same size as one light chain","Cytokines are larger because they carry two heavy chains each"],"e":"Antibodies are glycoproteins with a minimum molecular weight of around 150,000 Da (150 kDa), large for a protein, and their size is linked to their function. Cytokines are about 30 kDa: they are small, mobile signalling molecules that act as ligands on leukocyte receptors and need to be small to move around.","w":["This reverses the sizes: antibodies are the larger molecules.","Cytokines are small precisely because they must move around freely.","About 50 kDa is the weight of one heavy chain; the light chain is about 25 kDa.","Heavy chains belong to antibodies, not cytokines."],"k":"Antibody ~150 kDa vs cytokine ~30 kDa.","rn":[["","How big are cytokines compared with antibodies?","How big are cytokines compared with antibodies?","Cytokines about 30 kDa (kilodaltons); Antibodies about 150 kDa"],["","What is the minimum molecular weight of an antibody?","What is the minimum molecular weight of an antibody?","About 150 kDa (150,000 Da)"]]},{"id":37002,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"Which statement about the specificity of antibodies is correct?","o":["Each antibody binds one specific antigen, though the scaffold under the binding head can change over time","Each antibody binds every antigen on a pathogen's surface equally well","A single B cell makes many different antibodies to many antigens","The antigen bound by a B cell's antibodies changes to a new target as the cell ages and matures in the spleen","Antibodies bind antigen exactly as an enzyme binds its substrate"],"e":"Each antibody binds a single specific antigen. Through its lifetime each B lymphocyte produces antibodies that bind the same antigen, but the exact structure changes in terms of the scaffold on which the antigen-binding head sits. The lecturer compared binding to lock and key but stressed it is not exactly like an enzyme binding its substrate.","w":["Antibodies are specific: one antibody, one antigen.","Each B cell makes many identical copies of one antibody.","The antigen bound stays the same; only the scaffold changes.","Binding is lock-and-key-like but not exactly like enzyme–substrate binding."],"k":"One antibody, one antigen; the scaffold, not the target, changes over a B cell's life.","rn":[["","Over a B cell's lifetime, what changes about its antibody?","Over a B cell's lifetime, what changes about its antibody?","The scaffold the antigen-binding head sits on"]]},{"id":37003,"t":"What Is an Antibody","a":["epitope","an epitope","antigenic determinant"],"s":"What is the name for the part of an antigen to which an antibody binds?","e":"The epitope is the part of an antigen that the antibody binds. An antigen may be large, even a whole pathogen molecule, but the antibody's variable region interacts only with the epitope.","k":"Epitope = the part of an antigen bound by the antibody.","rn":[["","Epitope","Epitope","Part of an antigen the antibody binds"]]},{"id":37004,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"A patient's serum contains antibodies against thousands of different antigens. Which explanation best accounts for this diversity?","o":["Billions of different B cells each make copies of their own unique antibody","Each individual B cell makes a mixture of thousands of different antibodies at the same time","One B cell switches to a new antigen every few hours","Antibodies are copied from the pathogen's own genes","Cytokines bind the antigens and convert themselves into antibodies"],"e":"There are billions of different B lymphocytes, each theoretically capable of producing an antibody to a different antigen. Each B cell makes many identical copies of its specific antibody, which differs in structure from those of other B cells, so millions of different antibodies exist, covering every possible antigen.","w":["Each B cell makes many identical copies of one antibody.","A B cell's antibodies always bind the same antigen.","Antibodies are made by B cells from host genes rearranged by recombination.","Cytokines are separate signalling molecules and do not become antibodies."],"k":"Antibody diversity = many B cells, each with one unique antibody.","rn":[["","What does each single B cell make?","What does each single B cell make?","Many identical copies of one antibody; All bind the same antigen"]]},{"id":37005,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"During development, a B cell is found to make antibody that binds a host protein. What normally happens to it, and what may follow if this control is lost?","o":["It is destroyed or suppressed; loss of control can cause autoimmune disease","It is expanded into plasma cells; loss of control causes immunodeficiency","It switches class to IgE in the bone marrow; loss of this control causes allergy and asthma","It becomes a memory cell; loss of control causes graft rejection","It is sent to the spleen to mature; loss of control has no effect"],"e":"B cells that make antibodies able to bind self-antigens are either destroyed or suppressed from growing and producing antibody. This matters because antibodies against self would be disastrous. Occasionally the control is lost and antibodies to self are released, which can result in autoimmune disease.","w":["Self-reactive B cells are removed, not expanded; loss of control causes autoimmunity.","Class switching to IgE is not the fate of a self-reactive B cell.","Self-reactive cells are removed, not kept as memory cells.","Self-reactive cells are eliminated before release, and loss of control matters."],"k":"Self-reactive B cells are destroyed/suppressed; failure → autoimmune disease.","rn":[["","What can happen if control over self-reactive B cells is lost?","What can happen if control over self-reactive B cells is lost?","Autoimmune disease"]]},{"id":37006,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"A child is infected with a virus for the first time. Why is there not enough antibody to neutralise it straight away?","o":["Virus-specific B cells are very few before the first encounter","B cells cannot make antibody until they have been in the thymus","All B cells must first switch from IgG to IgM","Antibodies are only made after the virus is cleared","The virus destroys every B cell that could recognise it"],"e":"Before the first encounter, virus-specific B cells are very few and cannot release enough antibody to neutralise the virus as soon as it enters. After recognition and clonal expansion there is enough antibody in circulation to neutralise the virus as soon as it is met a second time.","w":["The thymus is for T cells; B cells develop in the bone marrow and mature in the spleen.","Switching goes from IgM to IgG, never back.","Antibody is secreted during infection, not only after clearance.","The lecture explains the delay by low numbers, not destruction of B cells."],"k":"Few specific B cells exist before first exposure.","rn":[["","Why is first-time antibody not enough to neutralise a virus at once?","Why is first-time antibody not enough to neutralise a virus at once?","Virus-specific B cells are very few; They release too little antibody"]]},{"id":37007,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"A B cell carrying a BCR that binds a virus is stimulated. Which TWO outcomes follow?","o":["It secretes soluble antibody and divides into identical clones","It becomes a T helper cell and secretes cytokines","It loses its BCR and dies by apoptosis immediately","It switches back from IgG to IgM and stops dividing until a second exposure","It migrates to the thymus and becomes a memory T cell"],"e":"On meeting the virus, B cells that carry a matching BCR start to secrete soluble anti-viral antibodies into the blood, and begin to divide, producing many identical copies of themselves (clones), each able to secrete the same virus-neutralising antibody. This cell division is clonal expansion.","w":["B cells do not become T helper cells.","Activation leads to proliferation and survival, not immediate death.","Switching back from IgG to IgM is not possible.","B cells do not migrate to the thymus to become T cells."],"k":"Activated B cell: secretes antibody + clonal expansion.","rn":[["","What 2 things happen when a B cell with a BCR (B cell receptor) meets its virus?","What 2 things happen when a B cell with a BCR (B cell receptor) meets its virus?","It secretes soluble antibody into the blood; It divides into identical clones"]]},{"id":37008,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"After a viral infection is cleared, the number of virus-specific B cells falls. What remains, and why does it matter?","o":["Memory cells with antibody on their membrane, making a repeat attack faster and stronger","Plasma cells that live for the rest of life and keep secreting antibody at peak levels","Naive B cells that have forgotten the virus completely","Only free antibody, with no specific cells left","T cells that produce antibody in place of B cells"],"e":"Once the virus is destroyed, virus-specific B cells reduce in number, but some remain as recognition B cells with virus-specific antibody on their membrane. These memory cells stay in circulation, and their increased number means a repeat invasion is met faster and more strongly; some can become antibody-secreting cells again.","w":["Plasma cells are short-lived antibody factories.","Memory cells retain recognition of the virus.","Specific memory B cells remain in circulation.","T cells do not produce antibody."],"k":"Memory B cells remain, so repeat infection is met faster and harder.","rn":[["","Why is the attack faster and stronger when the same virus returns?","Why is the attack faster and stronger when the same virus returns?","More memory cells now exist; Clonal expansion left enough antibody in the blood"]]},{"id":37009,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Which description of the antibody molecule is correct?","o":["Two identical heavy chains (~50 kDa) and two identical light chains (~25 kDa) joined by disulphide bonds","One heavy chain and one light chain joined by ionic bonds","Four identical polypeptide chains of 25 kDa each, held together by hydrogen bonds only, with no covalent links","Two heavy chains of 25 kDa and two light chains of 50 kDa","A single chain folded into a Y shape weighing about 30 kDa"],"e":"An antibody has four polypeptide chains: two identical heavy chains of about 50 kDa and two identical light chains of about 25 kDa, covalently linked by inter-chain disulphide bonds, giving about 150 kDa in total.","w":["There are two heavy and two light chains, linked covalently by disulphide bonds.","The chains are not identical in size and are linked by disulphide bonds.","Heavy chains are ~50 kDa and light chains ~25 kDa.","An antibody has four chains and is about 150 kDa."],"k":"Antibody: 2 H (~50 kDa) + 2 L (~25 kDa), disulphide-linked, ~150 kDa.","rn":[["","Are the two heavy chains of an antibody alike?","Are the two heavy chains of an antibody alike?","Yes, the two heavy chains are identical; The two light chains are also identical"],["","What links the antibody chains together?","What links the antibody chains together?","Inter-chain disulphide bonds (covalent)"],["","What is the molecular weight of each light chain?","What is the molecular weight of each light chain?","About 25 kDa"]]},{"id":37010,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Where is glycosylation of antibody chains carried out, and how much does it affect antigen binding?","o":["In the ER and/or Golgi; it rarely affects binding by the Fv domain","In the nucleus, where the added sugars determine which antigen the Fv domain will bind","In the lysosome; it destroys the antigen-binding site","In the mitochondria; it doubles antigen affinity","On the cell surface; it creates the epitope"],"e":"Antibody chains may be modified after translation by the addition of sugars (glycosylation) in the endoplasmic reticulum and/or Golgi complex. Such modifications rarely play a role in antigen binding via the Fv domain.","w":["Glycosylation occurs in the ER and/or Golgi, not the nucleus, and rarely affects binding.","Lysosomes are not described as the site and glycosylation does not destroy the binding site.","Mitochondria are not the site, and glycosylation rarely affects binding.","The epitope is on the antigen, not created by antibody glycosylation."],"k":"Glycosylation: ER/Golgi; rarely affects Fv binding.","rn":[["","What role does glycosylation play in antigen binding?","What role does glycosylation play in antigen binding?","Rarely any role via the Fv (variable fragment) domain"]]},{"id":37011,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"How does the B cell receptor (BCR) on a naive B cell differ from the soluble antibody the same cell later secretes?","o":["The BCR has an extra transmembrane domain; the antigen-binding site is identical","The BCR has a different antigen-binding site that later changes","The BCR lacks light chains, which are added before secretion","The BCR is always an IgG molecule, while the antibody that the same cell secretes is always IgD","The BCR binds many antigens, while the soluble form binds one"],"e":"In a naive (non-activated) B cell, the antibody includes an additional transmembrane domain that anchors it on the cell surface; in this format it is the BCR. After activation, gene rearrangements allow secretion of a soluble antibody form of the BCR. The structure of the antigen-binding site remains identical throughout.","w":["The antigen-binding site stays identical.","Both forms have the full four-chain structure.","The first antibodies made are mainly IgM, and the lecture does not define BCR versus soluble forms by IgG and IgD.","Both forms bind the same single antigen."],"k":"BCR = membrane-anchored antibody; same binding site as the secreted form.","rn":[["","BCR vs soluble antibody: is the antigen binding site different?","BCR vs soluble antibody: is the antigen binding site different?","No, identical throughout"],["","What extra domain does the antibody have in a naive B cell?","What extra domain does the antibody have in a naive B cell?","Transmembrane domain; Anchors it on the B cell surface"]]},{"id":37012,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"Which two events are needed for a mature B cell to undergo BCR gene rearrangement and secrete soluble antibody?","o":["Antigen cross-linking of the BCR plus cytokine stimulation by T helper cells","Binding of complement plus phagocytosis by a macrophage","Loss of the transmembrane domain plus migration to the thymus","Class switching to IgE plus mast cell activation","Antigen binding to Fc receptors on the B cell plus the release of histamine from mast cells"],"e":"Once the BCR is activated by antigen cross-linking and additional cytokine-mediated stimulation by T helper cells, the mature B cell undergoes BCR gene rearrangements that allow secretion of a soluble antibody. The lecturer stressed that both events are needed: a stable antigen–BCR interaction and the action of T helper cells.","w":["Complement and phagocytosis are not the two triggers described.","B cells do not migrate to the thymus.","Class switching to IgE and mast cells are not the activation triggers.","Fc receptors are on phagocytes, not the triggering BCR event."],"k":"Antigen cross-linking + T helper cytokines → rearrangement → secreted antibody.","rn":[["","What 2 events activate the BCR so the B cell can secrete antibody?","What 2 events activate the BCR so the B cell can secrete antibody?","Antigen cross-linking; Cytokine stimulation by T helper cells"]]},{"id":37013,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"In this figure, what does marker 5 indicate on the left-hand molecule?","o":["The plasma membrane that anchors the BCR","The antigen-binding site at the arm tip","The disulfide bonds linking the chains","The F<sub>v</sub> domain of the soluble antibody","The light chain of the antibody"],"e":"The left molecule is the surface antibody (BCR) anchored into the plasma membrane by its transmembrane domain; the right is the soluble antibody with no anchor. Labelled parts include the antigen-binding site at each arm tip, the disulfide bonds, the light chain (outer part of each arm), the heavy chain (inner, forming the stem) and the F<sub>v</sub> domain.","w":["The antigen-binding site is at the tips of the arms.","Disulfide bonds link the chains near the hinge.","The F<sub>v</sub> domain is labelled on the soluble antibody.","The light chain is the outer part of each arm."],"k":"The BCR is anchored in the plasma membrane; soluble antibody has no anchor.","f":"bc06","rn":[["","What is the membrane-bound form of the antibody called?","What is the membrane-bound form of the antibody called?","B cell receptor (BCR)"]]},{"id":37014,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Which region of an antibody determines its class (IgG, IgD, IgE, IgA or IgM)?","o":["The heavy chain constant region","The light chain variable region","The hypervariable regions at the arm tips","The disulfide bonds between chains","The glycosylation sites added in the Golgi"],"e":"Antibodies differ a lot in their variable regions, where they bind antigen epitopes, but much less in their constant region, which does not bind antigen. There is still some variation in the constant region: the heavy chain constant region determines which class an antibody belongs to.","w":["The light chain variable region helps form the antigen-binding site.","Hypervariable regions differ between antibodies to bind different antigens.","Disulfide bonds hold the chains together but do not define the class.","Glycosylation rarely affects binding and does not define the class."],"k":"Heavy chain constant region sets the antibody class.","rn":[["","What determines the antibody class?","What determines the antibody class?","Heavy chain (constant region)"]]},{"id":37015,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Two antibodies bind completely different antigens but are both IgG. Which statement best explains this?","o":["Their variable regions differ, but their heavy chain constant regions are of the same class","Their constant regions differ in class, but their variable regions are identical in sequence","They have identical structures and differ only in glycosylation","They differ only in the number of disulfide bonds","Their light chains are of different classes"],"e":"The variable (V) region differs a lot between antibodies so they can bind many different antigens; the hypervariable regions are where they differ. The constant region varies little and does not bind antigen, but the heavy chain constant region sets the class. So two IgG antibodies share a class-defining constant region but have different variable regions.","w":["Identical variable regions would bind the same antigen.","Antigen specificity comes from the variable region, not glycosylation.","Disulfide bonds do not determine antigen binding.","The class is set by the heavy chain, not the light chain."],"k":"Different variable regions + same heavy constant class = same isotype, different antigens.","rn":[["","What determines the antibody class?","What determines the antibody class?","Heavy chain (constant region)"],["","What is the constant region?","What is the constant region?","Little variation; Does not bind antigen; Heavy chain part decides the class"],["","What are the 2 regions of an antibody?","What are the 2 regions of an antibody?","Variable (V); Constant (C)"]]},{"id":37016,"t":"Antibody Structure","a":["hypervariable regions","hypervariable region","hypervariable","complementarity determining regions","cdr","cdrs"],"s":"Which parts of the antibody variable domain differ most between antibodies and form the contact with the antigen?","e":"The hypervariable regions differ between antibodies. They lie in the variable domains at the arm tips and form the antigen-binding site that contacts the epitope.","k":"Hypervariable regions differ between antibodies and bind antigen.","rn":[["","Which regions differ most between antibodies?","Which regions differ most between antibodies?","Hypervariable regions"]]},{"id":37017,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"An antibody binds strongly and specifically to its antigen. Which outcome is NOT listed in the speaker notes as something this can signal?","o":["Release of histamine by bradykinin","Activation of the complement system to kill bacteria","Activation of macrophages to engulf viruses and bacteria","Involvement in activating T cells that kill cells with non-self proteins","Engulfment of bacteria by activated macrophages"],"e":"The speaker notes say that antigen binding can signal activation of the complement system (to kill bacteria) or of macrophages (to engulf viruses and bacteria), and that antibodies are also involved in activating T cells, which can kill a cell expressing proteins recognised as non-self (e.g. cancer cells). Histamine and bradykinin are not part of this list.","w":["Complement activation is listed.","Macrophage activation is listed.","Involvement in T cell activation is listed.","Macrophage engulfment of bacteria is listed."],"k":"Antibody binding can signal complement, macrophages and T cell activation."},{"id":37018,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"On this labelled antibody, which label indicates the hypervariable regions?","o":["2","1","3","5","7"],"e":"The labels mark: 1 the antigen-binding site; 2 the hypervariable regions; 3 the antigen; 4 the epitope; 5 the light chain; 6 the heavy chain; 7 the key (V variable, C constant, SS disulfide bonds); 8 heavy chains determining the class. The hypervariable regions sit at the tips of the variable domains.","w":["1 marks the antigen-binding site as a whole.","3 marks the antigen.","5 marks the light chain.","7 marks the key to the symbols."],"k":"Draw and label: binding site, hypervariable regions, epitope, light and heavy chains.","f":"bc07","rn":[["","Which regions differ most between antibodies?","Which regions differ most between antibodies?","Hypervariable regions"],["","What is the variable region?","What is the variable region?","Binds the antigen epitope; Differs a lot between antibodies"]]},{"id":37019,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Which pairing of antibody class and structure is correct?","o":["IgA: dimer","IgM: monomer","IgG: pentamer","IgE: dimer","IgD: pentamer"],"e":"IgG, IgD and IgE are quite similar monomers. IgA is a dimer and IgM is a pentamer of five antibody units. The extra units give IgA and IgM more antigen-binding arms.","w":["IgM is a pentamer.","IgG is a monomer.","IgE is a monomer.","IgD is a monomer."],"k":"IgG/IgD/IgE monomers; IgA dimer; IgM pentamer.","rn":[["","Which antibody class is a dimer?","Which antibody class is a dimer?","IgA"]]},{"id":37020,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"A mucosal swab from the gut contains an antibody class adapted to protect mucosal surfaces. Which class is it most likely to be?","o":["IgA","IgG","IgD","IgE","IgM"],"e":"The speaker notes give the key roles: IgG most abundant and circulating in blood; IgM very efficient in the early response; IgA at mucosal surfaces; IgD on the surface of B cells; IgE in allergies and asthma. Secretory IgA is a dimer that crosses mucus membranes.","w":["IgG is the most abundant antibody in blood.","IgD is on the surface of B cells.","IgE is involved in allergies and asthma.","IgM is very efficient in the early response."],"k":"IgA protects mucosal surfaces.","rn":[["","Where is IgA found?","Where is IgA found?","Secretory tissues / mucosal surfaces; e.g. gut, respiratory tract"]]},{"id":37021,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which class makes up the majority of total serum immunoglobulins?","o":["IgG","IgM","IgA","IgD","IgE"],"e":"IgG is the most abundant immunoglobulin and makes up the majority of total serum immunoglobulins; it circulates in blood. IgM is the early pentamer, IgA protects mucosa, IgD sits on B cells and IgE is involved in allergy.","w":["IgM is very efficient early but not the most abundant.","IgA protects mucosal surfaces.","IgD is a lymphocyte-bound receptor.","IgE is linked to allergy and parasites."],"k":"IgG = majority of serum immunoglobulins.","rn":[["","IgG: key characteristic?","IgG: key characteristic?","Most abundant immunoglobulin; Circulates in blood"],["","What do IgA, IgD, IgE, IgG and IgM stand for?","What do IgA, IgD, IgE, IgG and IgM stand for?","Immunoglobulin A, D, E, G and M"]]},{"id":37022,"t":"Antibody Structure","a":["igm","immunoglobulin m"],"s":"Which antibody class is a pentamer?","e":"IgM is a pentamer: five antibody units joined by a J chain, giving 10 antigen-binding sites. It is one of the first antibodies made in a new infection.","k":"IgM = pentamer.","rn":[["","Which antibody class is a pentamer?","Which antibody class is a pentamer?","IgM"],["","Image card: Which antibody is this? How many antigen binding sites?","Which antibody is this? How many antigen binding sites?","IgM (pentamer); 10"]]},{"id":37023,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"In this figure, which labelled molecule is shown as two units joined together?","o":["IgA","IgG","IgD","IgE","IgM"],"e":"The figure shows IgG, IgD and IgE as single Y-shaped monomers on the top row. On the bottom row, IgA is shown as two units joined by a J chain (a dimer) and IgM as five units joined by a J chain (a pentamer).","w":["IgG is a single monomer.","IgD is a single monomer.","IgE is a single monomer.","IgM has five units."],"k":"IgA dimer; IgM pentamer.","f":"bi07","rn":[["","Which antibody class is a dimer?","Which antibody class is a dimer?","IgA"],["","How many antigen sites per molecule: IgG vs IgA vs IgM?","How many antigen sites per molecule: IgG vs IgA vs IgM?","IgG 2; IgA 4; IgM 10"]]},{"id":37024,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"In the IgG space-filling model, how many constant (C) domains does each chain have?","o":["Each light chain has 1; each heavy chain has 3","Each light chain has 3; each heavy chain has 1","Both chains have 2 constant domains each","Light chains have none; heavy chains have 4","Both chains have 1 constant domain each"],"e":"In the space-filling model, the variable and constant domains of the IgG heavy and light chains are shown in different colours. Each light chain has one C domain, whilst each heavy chain has three C domains (C<sub>H</sub>1, C<sub>H</sub>2, C<sub>H</sub>3).","w":["This reverses the numbers.","The chains have different numbers of C domains.","Light chains have one C domain, heavy chains three.","Heavy chains have three C domains."],"k":"IgG: L chain 1 C domain; H chain 3 C domains.","rn":[["","How many C domains does each light chain have?","How many C domains does each light chain have?","One"],["","How many C domains does each heavy chain have?","How many C domains does each heavy chain have?","Three"],["","What does a valency of 1 mean?","What does a valency of 1 mean?","One single four-chain molecule; 2 heavy and 2 light chains; Each with two antigen binding domains"]]},{"id":37025,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"What does the label \"V<sub>L</sub>\" mean in antibody nomenclature?","o":["The variable domain of the light chain","The variable domain of the heavy chain","The constant domain of the light chain","The valency of the antibody","The first constant domain of the heavy chain"],"e":"Antibody labelling uses V or C for variable or constant domain, with subscript H for heavy chain and L for light chain. So V<sub>L</sub> is the variable domain of the light chain, V<sub>H</sub> the variable domain of the heavy chain, and C<sub>H</sub>1 the first constant domain of the heavy chain.","w":["That would be V<sub>H</sub>.","That would be C<sub>L</sub>.","Valency is the number of binding sites, not a domain label.","That would be C<sub>H</sub>1."],"k":"V/C + subscript H/L names antibody domains.","rn":[["","What do the letters V, C, H and L mean in domain names like VH or CL?","What do the letters V, C, H and L mean in domain names like VH or CL?","V variable; C constant; H heavy chain; L light chain"],["","Name the domains of an IgG light chain.","Name the domains of an IgG light chain.","VL (variable); CL (constant)"],["","How many C domains does each light chain have?","How many C domains does each light chain have?","One"]]},{"id":37026,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Why is the antibody often drawn as a simple \"Y\"?","o":["It is a convenient simplification; the real structure is far more complex","It is an exact representation of the 3D structure","Because all antibodies are pentamers","Because the binding site is at the stem of the Y","Because antibodies have no constant domains"],"e":"Whilst it is convenient to depict an antibody as the letter Y, the actual structure is far more complex: the lecturer described it as more like a squashed capital Y. The binding sites are at the arm tips, and both variable and constant domains are present.","w":["The Y is a simplification, not an exact representation.","Only IgM is a pentamer.","The binding sites are at the arm tips.","Antibodies do have constant domains."],"k":"The Y shape is a simplification of a more complex structure.","rn":[["","Is the letter \"Y\" an accurate picture of IgG?","Is the letter \"Y\" an accurate picture of IgG?","No, convenient only; Real structure is far more complex"]]},{"id":37027,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"In this IgG model, which label marks the carbohydrate chain?","o":["5","1","2","6","7"],"e":"The IgG model labels: 1 antigen-binding site; 2 V<sub>H</sub> domain; 3 V<sub>L</sub> domain; 4 C<sub>L</sub> and C<sub>H</sub>1 domains; 5 carbohydrate chain; 6 heavy chains; 7 C<sub>H</sub>3 at the base. The tips (V domains) bind antigen and the body (C domains) is constant.","w":["1 is the antigen-binding site.","2 is the V<sub>H</sub> domain.","6 marks the heavy chains.","7 is C<sub>H</sub>3 at the base."],"k":"The IgG model shows V and C domains and a carbohydrate chain.","f":"bc08","rn":[["","What is the carbohydrate chain on IgG?","What is the carbohydrate chain on IgG?","Sugar attached by glycosylation"],["","How many C domains does each heavy chain have?","How many C domains does each heavy chain have?","Three"]]},{"id":37028,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","a":["affinity"],"s":"What term describes how strong a single binding interaction is, for example one Fab arm binding its antigen?","e":"Affinity describes how strong an interaction is between two molecules, such as a receptor and its ligand, an enzyme and substrate, or one antibody binding site and its antigen. It is also called intrinsic affinity, as opposed to functional affinity (avidity).","k":"Affinity = strength of a single binding interaction.","rn":[["","Affinity","Affinity","Strength of binding; Of one antigen binding site"],["","Which antibody forms are described by affinity, and which by avidity?","Which antibody forms are described by affinity, and which by avidity?","Fab and single-arm IgG: affinity; Bivalent IgG and IgM: avidity"],["","What is intrinsic affinity?","What is intrinsic affinity?","Affinity of one single binding site; e.g. Fab, or IgG using one arm"]]},{"id":37029,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"A single Fab fragment binds its antigen with high affinity. Why can a pathogen captured by a single Fab still escape?","o":["Binding is reversible: on rate is seconds but off rate is tens of minutes","Fab fragments cannot bind antigen at all","A Fab binds only self antigens","A Fab has ten binding sites that compete with each other","Fab binding is permanent, but the pathogen divides"],"e":"When a single antibody binding domain such as a Fab fragment binds antigen, affinity can be high, but the association is reversible and the complex can fall apart. For a Fab fragment the on rate is a few seconds and the off rate tens of minutes. So binding is quick but not permanent, and a captured pathogen can escape.","w":["A Fab is one arm of the antibody and does bind antigen.","A Fab binds its specific foreign antigen, not self antigens.","A Fab has a single binding site.","Fab binding is reversible, not permanent."],"k":"Fab: high affinity but reversible (on seconds, off tens of minutes).","rn":[["","Fab fragment: how long is the \"off\" rate?","Fab fragment: how long is the \"off\" rate?","Tens of minutes"]]},{"id":37030,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"Why does a whole IgG molecule stay bound to a pathogen much longer than a single Fab?","o":["The chance of both arms detaching at the same time is low","IgG has a much higher intrinsic affinity per arm","IgG binds covalently to the pathogen surface","IgG has ten arms that never release","IgG converts the pathogen into a self antigen"],"e":"An intact antibody has two antigen-binding domains, and each binds independently with its own on and off rates. Both arms bind quickly, but the probability that both detach at the same time is low, so the effective binding time may be hours or days. This functional affinity is avidity.","w":["The advantage comes from two arms (avidity), not higher affinity per arm.","Antibody binding is reversible, not covalent.","IgG has two arms; IgM has ten.","Binding does not change the pathogen into a self antigen."],"k":"Two arms rarely detach together → long effective binding (avidity).","rn":[["","Why does an intact IgG stay bound longer than a Fab?","Why does an intact IgG stay bound longer than a Fab?","Both arms unlikely to detach at once"]]},{"id":37031,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"By up to how much can the functional affinity (avidity) of a bivalent antibody exceed that of a single binding domain, and what is the figure for IgM?","o":["Up to 1000-fold for bivalent; up to 10-million-fold for IgM","Up to 10-fold for bivalent; up to 100-fold for IgM","Up to 1000-fold for both bivalent antibodies and IgM","Up to 10-million-fold for bivalent; up to 1000-fold for IgM","There is no advantage: avidity equals affinity in every antibody"],"e":"A bivalent antibody has a functional affinity (avidity) that can be up to 1000-fold (10<super>3</super>) greater than a single antigen-binding domain. In IgM, with ten binding domains, the effect is magnified, and the enhancement can be up to 10-million-fold (10<super>7</super>). However, IgM is energetically expensive for B cells to produce.","w":["These numbers understate the advantage of multivalence.","IgM's ten arms magnify the effect far beyond 1000-fold.","This swaps the bivalent and IgM figures.","Multivalence gives a large advantage, which is the definition of avidity."],"k":"Avidity: bivalent up to 10<super>3</super>-fold; IgM up to 10<super>7</super>-fold.","rn":[["","How much enhancement does IgM's avidity give?","How much enhancement does IgM's avidity give?","Up to 10-million-fold (10^7)"],["","How much greater is avidity of a bivalent antibody than a single binding site?","How much greater is avidity of a bivalent antibody than a single binding site?","Up to 1,000-fold (10^3)"]]},{"id":37032,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"AI simulations suggested the ideal antibody would have high affinity and a particular number of binding domains. Which conclusion followed?","o":["Two to three domains is ideal, so IgG with two appears perfectly evolved","Ten domains is ideal, so IgM with its pentamer of binding arms is the most efficient antibody","One domain is ideal, so Fab fragments outperform whole antibodies","Five domains is ideal, so IgM should be a monomer","Four domains is ideal, so secretory IgA is the perfect antibody"],"e":"AI simulations showed that the ideal antibody would have a high affinity for an antigen and between two and three binding domains. Since only whole numbers of arms are possible, IgG, with two arms, appears perfectly evolved for its function. IgM has ten domains but is energetically expensive.","w":["IgM has ten domains but is energetically expensive; the ideal was two to three.","A single Fab falls apart easily; the ideal was two to three.","IgM is a pentamer and the ideal number was two to three.","Secretory IgA has four arms; the ideal was two to three."],"k":"Ideal antibody: high affinity + 2–3 binding domains → IgG.","rn":[["","Which antibody seems perfectly evolved for its function?","Which antibody seems perfectly evolved for its function?","IgG (2 binding arms)"],["","According to AI simulations, how many binding domains does the ideal antibody have?","According to AI simulations, how many binding domains does the ideal antibody have?","2 to 3; With high affinity"]]},{"id":37033,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"In the valence table, how are a Fab and an IgG binding with both arms each described?","o":["Fab: affinity (intrinsic); IgG with two arms: avidity (functional affinity)","Fab: avidity (functional affinity); IgG with two arms: affinity (intrinsic affinity)","Both are described as intrinsic affinity","Both are described as functional affinity","Fab: valence 2; IgG with two arms: valence 10"],"e":"The valence table lists effective antibody valence (Fab 1, IgG 1 or 2, IgM up to 10), antigen valence (1, 1, n, n), the advantage of multivalence (–, –, 10<super>3</super>-fold, 10<super>7</super>-fold) and the definition of binding: affinity (intrinsic affinity) for Fab and an IgG using one arm, avidity (functional affinity) for IgG using two arms and IgM.","w":["This reverses the definitions.","An IgG using both arms shows avidity, not intrinsic affinity.","A Fab shows intrinsic affinity, not functional affinity.","Fab valence is 1 and IgG valence is 2; up to 10 is IgM."],"k":"One site = intrinsic affinity; multiple sites = functional affinity (avidity).","rn":[["","What is intrinsic affinity?","What is intrinsic affinity?","Affinity of one single binding site; e.g. Fab, or IgG using one arm"],["","Which antibody forms are described by affinity, and which by avidity?","Which antibody forms are described by affinity, and which by avidity?","Fab and single-arm IgG: affinity; Bivalent IgG and IgM: avidity"]]},{"id":37034,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","a":["avidity","functional affinity"],"s":"What term describes the overall binding strength produced by several binding sites working together?","e":"Avidity, also called functional affinity, describes the overall strength of several antigen-binding sites binding together and how long the antibody stays bound. A bivalent antibody's avidity can be up to 1000-fold greater than one binding domain.","k":"Avidity = functional affinity of multiple sites.","rn":[["","Avidity","Avidity","Functional affinity; Combined strength of multiple binding sites"]]},{"id":37035,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"The speaker notes compare avidity to Velcro. What is the point of the analogy?","o":["Each hook adds little, but many small binding events add up to a strong hold","Velcro binds only once and can never be reattached","Each individual hook is as strong as the whole strip, so numbers do not matter","Velcro shows that antibodies bind covalently","It shows that only one arm of an antibody matters"],"e":"A good analogy is Velcro: each individual hook and eye contributes little to the strength of binding, but numerous small binding events add up to a strong (avid) interaction overall, which is functional affinity. This is why IgM's pentamer compensates for low affinity early in infection.","w":["Velcro reattaches repeatedly, like antibody arms.","Individual hooks are weak; strength comes from numbers.","Antibody binding is reversible, not covalent.","The point is that many binding sites together matter."],"k":"Velcro analogy: many weak bonds → strong avid binding.","rn":[["","What analogy explains avidity?","What analogy explains avidity?","Velcro; Many weak hooks add up to strong binding"]]},{"id":37036,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"In this figure, what does the label 4 show compared with label 1?","o":["Bivalent binding, where one arm can let go while the other holds on","Univalent binding that falls off just as easily as the antibody at label 1 does","IgM binding with ten arms at once","A Fab binding with higher intrinsic affinity","Complement binding to the antigen"],"e":"The figure shows univalent binding (one arm bound) switching between ON and OFF, so the antibody easily falls off. Bivalent binding uses both arms: one arm can let go while the other holds on, so the antibody rarely detaches fully. The table on the right lists Fab, IgG with one or two arms, and IgM.","w":["Bivalent binding is much more stable than univalent binding.","The bivalent panel shows two arms, not IgM's ten.","A Fab is a single arm, shown in the univalent panel.","Complement is not shown in this figure."],"k":"Bivalent binding rarely releases fully because one arm holds on.","f":"bc09","rn":[["","Which antibody forms are described by affinity, and which by avidity?","Which antibody forms are described by affinity, and which by avidity?","Fab and single-arm IgG: affinity; Bivalent IgG and IgM: avidity"],["","Univalent vs bivalent binding?","Univalent vs bivalent binding?","Univalent: one binding site; Bivalent: two binding sites"]]},{"id":37037,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"What largely explains the differences in function between the five antibody isotypes?","o":["The 3D structure of the heavy chain constant region","The 3D structure of the light chain variable region","The antigen each isotype binds","The number of hypervariable regions","Whether the antibody is made in the thymus or bone marrow"],"e":"The variation in function of the five isotypes is largely a result of differences in the three-dimensional structure of the heavy chain constant region. These differences influence which immune system partner the antibody works with (e.g. macrophage receptors, complement protein), but NOT the antigen to which it binds.","w":["The light chain variable region shapes antigen binding, not isotype function.","Isotype does not determine which antigen is bound.","Hypervariable regions shape antigen binding.","Antibodies are made by B cells, not in the thymus."],"k":"Isotype function depends on heavy chain constant region structure.","rn":[["","What causes the variation in function of the 5 isotypes?","What causes the variation in function of the 5 isotypes?","3D structure of heavy chain constant region"]]},{"id":37038,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"A researcher swaps the constant region of an IgM antibody for an IgG constant region. What would change?","o":["The immune partners it works with, but not the antigen it binds","The antigen it binds, but not the immune partners such as macrophage receptors","Both the antigen and the immune partners","Neither the antigen nor any function","Only its glycosylation in the Golgi"],"e":"Differences in the heavy chain constant region affect function by influencing which immune system partner the antibody works with, such as antibody receptors on macrophages or complement proteins, but NOT the antigen to which it binds. This is the basis of class switching: same specificity, different effector function.","w":["Antigen binding is set by the variable region, which is unchanged.","The antigen stays the same; only effector partners change.","Constant region changes do change effector function.","Function changes, not just glycosylation."],"k":"Constant region swap: same antigen, different effector partners.","rn":[["","What do isotype differences affect?","What do isotype differences affect?","Which immune partner the antibody works with; e.g. macrophage receptors, complement"]]},{"id":37039,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"A pregnant woman has high levels of protective antibody against a pathogen. Which antibody class can cross the placenta to protect the fetus?","o":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgD","IgE"],"e":"In the properties table, only IgG crosses the placenta (++). The lecturer linked this to IgG being smaller than IgA and IgM, so newborns gain immunity early in life. IgA, IgM, IgD and IgE do not cross.","w":["IgA does not cross the placenta.","IgM, a pentamer, does not cross the placenta.","IgD does not cross the placenta.","IgE does not cross the placenta."],"k":"Only IgG crosses the placenta.","rn":[["","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","IgG"]]},{"id":37040,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which isotype binds mast cells and basophils and is involved in allergy symptoms and inflammation in parasitic infections?","o":["IgE","IgG","IgA","IgM","IgD"],"e":"In the properties table, only IgE binds mast cells and basophils (++). Its characteristics are initiation of inflammation in parasitic infections and involvement in allergy symptoms; the speaker notes link IgE to allergies and asthma.","w":["IgG binds macrophages best but not mast cells.","IgA protects mucosal surfaces.","IgM is best with complement.","IgD is a lymphocyte-bound receptor."],"k":"IgE: mast cells/basophils, allergy, parasites.","rn":[["","IgE: key characteristics?","IgE: key characteristics?","Inflammation in parasitic infections; Allergy symptoms; Asthma"],["","Which isotype binds mast cells and basophils?","Which isotype binds mast cells and basophils?","IgE"]]},{"id":37041,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which isotype interacts most strongly with complement system molecules?","o":["IgM","IgG","IgA","IgD","IgE"],"e":"In the properties table, IgM has +++ for interaction with complement, IgG ++, IgD +, and IgA and IgE –. IgM is described as very efficient against bacteria.","w":["IgG interacts with complement (++) but less than IgM.","IgA does not interact with complement in the table.","IgD has only + for complement.","IgE does not interact with complement in the table."],"k":"IgM is the strongest complement activator.","rn":[["","Which isotypes interact with complement, and how much?","Which isotypes interact with complement, and how much?","IgM +++; IgG ++; IgD +"]]},{"id":37042,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which isotype binds macrophages most strongly, supporting phagocytosis?","o":["IgG","IgM","IgA","IgD","IgE"],"e":"In the properties table, IgG binds macrophages most strongly (+++), followed by IgA and IgE (+), while IgM and IgD do not bind macrophages. The lecturer linked IgG's macrophage binding to phagocytosis.","w":["IgM does not bind macrophages in the table.","IgA binds macrophages only weakly (+).","IgD does not bind macrophages.","IgE binds macrophages only weakly (+)."],"k":"IgG binds macrophages best (+++).","rn":[["","Which isotypes bind macrophages?","Which isotypes bind macrophages?","IgG +++; IgA +; IgE +"]]},{"id":37043,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which statement about antigen binding across the five isotypes is correct?","o":["All five show the same level of antigen binding (++)","IgM binds antigen far better than any of the other four isotypes in the table","IgE cannot bind antigen at all","Only IgG binds antigen; the others bind cells","IgD binds antigen only after leaving the B cell"],"e":"In the properties table, antigen binding is ++ for every isotype. The lecturer stressed there is no difference between the classes in antigen binding; the differences lie in how they work within the immune system, set by the heavy chain constant region.","w":["IgM's advantage is avidity from many arms; the table gives ++ for all.","IgE does bind antigen (++).","All isotypes bind antigen.","IgD is a lymphocyte-bound receptor."],"k":"Antigen binding is equal (++) across isotypes.","rn":[["","Do the isotypes differ in antigen binding?","Do the isotypes differ in antigen binding?","No, all ++"],["","What do isotype differences NOT affect?","What do isotype differences NOT affect?","The antigen the antibody binds"],["","What happens to IgM and IgG levels once antigen is neutralised?","What happens to IgM and IgG levels once antigen is neutralised?","They fall over time"]]},{"id":37044,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which isotype has a valency of \"1 or 2\" in the properties table?","o":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgD","IgE"],"e":"The valency row lists IgG 1, IgA 1 or 2, IgM 5, IgD 1, IgE 1. A valency of one refers to a single four-chain molecule (two heavy and two light chains) with two antigen-binding domains. IgA can be a monomer or a dimer, and IgM is a pentamer.","w":["IgG has a valency of 1.","IgM has a valency of 5.","IgD has a valency of 1.","IgE has a valency of 1."],"k":"Valency: IgG 1, IgA 1–2, IgM 5, IgD 1, IgE 1.","rn":[["","What is the valency of each isotype?","What is the valency of each isotype?","IgG 1; IgA 1 or 2; IgM 5; IgD 1; IgE 1"]]},{"id":37045,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"What does a valency of 1 mean in the properties table?","o":["A single four-chain molecule with two antigen-binding domains","A single antigen-binding domain on one arm","A molecule that is able to bind only one antigen molecule in total at any time","One heavy chain without any light chains","A Fab fragment cut from the antibody"],"e":"The speaker notes explain that a valency of one refers to a single four-chain molecule (two heavy and two light chains), each with two antigen-binding domains. So IgM's valency of 5 means five such units.","w":["A single four-chain unit has two binding domains, not one.","A four-chain unit can bind two antigens.","The unit has two heavy and two light chains.","A Fab is one arm, not the valency-1 unit."],"k":"Valency 1 = one four-chain unit with two binding domains.","rn":[["","What does a valency of 1 mean?","What does a valency of 1 mean?","One single four-chain molecule; 2 heavy and 2 light chains; Each with two antigen binding domains"]]},{"id":37046,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","a":["igg","immunoglobulin g"],"s":"Name the only antibody isotype that crosses the placenta.","e":"IgG is the only isotype listed as crossing the placenta. It is the most abundant immunoglobulin and binds macrophages best.","k":"IgG crosses the placenta.","rn":[["","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","IgG"]]},{"id":37047,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Early in a new infection, a patient's first antibodies have not yet reached full affinity, yet they still hold antigen effectively. Which feature explains this?","o":["IgM's pentamer gives 10 binding sites, so avidity compensates","IgG's two arms give it the highest intrinsic affinity of any isotype at this early stage","IgE binds mast cells to hold antigen in place","IgA's secretory piece protects it from proteolysis","IgD is secreted in large amounts early on"],"e":"IgM exists as a pentamer of five antibody molecules linked by the J chain, giving 10 antigen-binding sites. Even if affinity is not yet fully developed, the avidity effect compensates. IgM is one of the first immunoglobulins produced in a new infection.","w":["IgG appears later, after class switching and affinity maturation.","IgE binding to mast cells is about allergy and parasites.","The secretory piece concerns IgA at mucosa.","IgD is described as a lymphocyte-bound receptor."],"k":"IgM: 10 binding sites; avidity compensates for low early affinity.","rn":[["","Why does IgM still bind well before affinity has matured?","Why does IgM still bind well before affinity has matured?","Avidity compensates; 10 binding sites"],["","Primary response: why does early IgM bind well despite low affinity?","Primary response: why does early IgM bind well despite low affinity?","Pentamer gives high avidity"]]},{"id":37048,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which statement correctly compares IgM and secretory IgA (sIgA)?","o":["Both use a J chain; only sIgA has a secretory piece","Both have a secretory piece; only IgM has a J chain","IgM is a dimer and sIgA is a pentamer","Both have ten antigen-binding sites","Neither has a J chain linking its units"],"e":"IgM is a pentamer of five antibody molecules linked by the J chain, giving 10 binding sites. Secretory IgA is a dimer of two antibody molecules linked by a J chain and a secretory piece that protects from proteolysis and aids secretion across mucus membranes, giving four binding arms.","w":["The secretory piece is unique to sIgA.","IgM is a pentamer and sIgA a dimer.","IgM has 10 sites; sIgA has 4.","Both use a J chain."],"k":"IgM: pentamer + J chain (10 sites). sIgA: dimer + J chain + secretory piece (4 sites).","rn":[["","What 2 structures link the units of sIgA?","What 2 structures link the units of sIgA?","J chain; Secretory piece"]]},{"id":37049,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Why is the secretory piece important for IgA in the gut?","o":["It protects IgA from proteolysis and helps secretion across mucus membranes","It increases the number of antigen-binding sites on the dimer to ten, like IgM","It lets IgA cross the placenta","It allows IgA to bind mast cells","It converts IgA into IgG"],"e":"Secretory IgA is a dimer joined by a J chain and a secretory piece. The secretory piece protects it from proteolysis (degradation) and facilitates secretion across mucus membranes, such as the gut or respiratory tract. The four binding arms increase avidity for extracellular pathogens.","w":["sIgA has four binding sites; ten belongs to IgM.","Only IgG crosses the placenta.","Binding mast cells is a property of IgE.","The secretory piece does not change the class."],"k":"Secretory piece: protects from proteolysis, aids mucosal secretion.","rn":[["","What are the 2 jobs of the secretory piece?","What are the 2 jobs of the secretory piece?","Protects from proteolysis; Helps secretion across mucous membranes"]]},{"id":37050,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","a":["j chain","joining chain","the j chain"],"s":"Name the polypeptide that links the units of IgM and IgA together.","e":"The J chain links the five units of IgM and the two units of secretory IgA. Secretory IgA additionally has a secretory piece.","k":"J chain links IgM and IgA units.","rn":[["","What links the 5 units of IgM together?","What links the 5 units of IgM together?","J chain"],["","What 2 structures link the units of sIgA?","What 2 structures link the units of sIgA?","J chain; Secretory piece"]]},{"id":37051,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"In this figure, which label marks the structure that protects the lower molecule from proteolysis?","o":["5","1","2","3","4"],"e":"The figure labels: 1 the pentameric IgM; 2 the J chain at its centre; 3 the dimeric secretory IgA; 4 the J chain joining the IgA units; 5 the secretory piece, which protects sIgA from proteolysis and aids secretion across mucus membranes.","w":["1 marks pentameric IgM.","2 marks the J chain of IgM.","3 marks the dimeric IgA as a whole.","4 marks the J chain of IgA."],"k":"The secretory piece of sIgA protects it from proteolysis.","f":"bc12","rn":[["","What are the 2 jobs of the secretory piece?","What are the 2 jobs of the secretory piece?","Protects from proteolysis; Helps secretion across mucous membranes"]]},{"id":37052,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"Why could antibody diversity not be achieved if one gene encoded the heavy chain and one gene the light chain?","o":["The same proteins would always be made, so the binding site could not vary","The light chain would then be too large to fold into its correct shape in the ER","Antibodies would become IgE automatically","Heavy chains would be unable to form disulphide bonds","B cells would migrate to the thymus instead"],"e":"If one gene encoded the heavy chain and one the light chain, the same proteins would always be formed, and antibodies could not change their epitope-binding site for the different antigens they meet. B cells solve this by rearranging their DNA (somatic recombination), which sets them apart from other cells.","w":["Folding size is not the problem described.","Class is not determined by gene number in this way.","Disulphide bonds are not the issue.","B cells do not migrate to the thymus."],"k":"Fixed single genes → no binding-site diversity.","rn":[["","Is the antigen binding domain germline encoded?","Is the antigen binding domain germline encoded?","No; Made by somatic recombination and affinity maturation"],["","What shape are antibodies drawn as?","What shape are antibodies drawn as?","Y-shaped proteins made by B cells"]]},{"id":37053,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"Which statement about the DNA of B cells is correct?","o":["B cells are the only cells whose DNA is not exactly the same as germline DNA","B cells keep DNA identical to all other somatic cells throughout their life","B cells lose their DNA after becoming plasma cells","All somatic cells rearrange their antibody genes","B cell DNA changes in its nucleotide sequence by methylation alone"],"e":"All somatic cells share the same genetic material and differ only in which proteins they express. B cells are the exception: they are the only cell type whose DNA is not exactly the same as germline DNA, because they rearrange antibody gene segments by somatic recombination.","w":["B cells rearrange their antibody genes, so their DNA differs.","Plasma cells keep their DNA.","Only B cells are described as rearranging antibody genes.","Methylation is epigenetic and does not change the nucleotide sequence."],"k":"B cells' DNA differs from germline DNA after rearrangement.","rn":[["","How is B cell DNA different from other somatic cells?","How is B cell DNA different from other somatic cells?","Not the same as germline DNA; Slightly altered"],["","What are the 3 stages of B cell gene rearrangement?","What are the 3 stages of B cell gene rearrangement?","Immature: germline DNA; Differentiating: rearranged DNA; Immunocompetent: mRNA, surface IgM"],["","What are the steps from germline DNA to light chain mRNA (messenger RNA)?","What are the steps from germline DNA to light chain mRNA (messenger RNA)?","Germline DNA; B cell DNA: V joined to J; Primary RNA transcript; mRNA: V, J and C joined"]]},{"id":37054,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"Which gene segments are spliced together to make an antibody heavy chain, and which segment is missing from the light chain?","o":["Heavy: V, D, J and C; the light chain lacks D","Heavy: V, J and C; the light chain lacks J","Heavy: V and C only; the light chain lacks V","Heavy: D, J and C; the light chain lacks C","Heavy and light chains both use V, D, J and C"],"e":"The antibody chains are assembled by splicing variable (V), diversity (D), joining (J) and constant (C) gene segments. The heavy chain is formed from V, D, J and C, whilst the light chain lacks D and uses V, J and C.","w":["The heavy chain includes D, and the light chain lacks D, not J.","Both chains use J segments.","The light chain has a C segment; it lacks D.","The light chain lacks D."],"k":"Heavy = VDJC; light = VJC (no D).","rn":[["","Which gene segments form the light chain?","Which gene segments form the light chain?","V, J and C (no D)"],["","What 4 polypeptide chains make up an antibody?","What 4 polypeptide chains make up an antibody?","2 heavy (H) chains; 2 light (L) chains"],["","Which gene segments form the heavy chain?","Which gene segments form the heavy chain?","V, D, J and C"]]},{"id":37055,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Which of the following is NOT listed as required for somatic recombination?","o":["Class switching from IgM to IgG","Recombination signal sequences","Recombination activation enzymes including RAG-1/2","Epigenetic changes such as methylation and acetylation","Changes to the structure of the DNA"],"e":"Somatic recombination requires recombination signal sequences, recombination activation enzymes (including RAG-1/2), and epigenetic changes to the DNA structure, including changes to methylation and acetylation. Class switching happens later in the immune response and is not a requirement for V(D)J recombination.","w":["Recombination signal sequences are required.","RAG-1/2 enzymes are required.","Methylation and acetylation changes are required.","Epigenetic changes alter DNA structure and are required."],"k":"V(D)J needs signal sequences, RAG-1/2 and epigenetic changes.","rn":[["","What makes IgG appear in the primary response?","What makes IgG appear in the primary response?","Class switching; Affinity maturation"],["","Is a switch back from IgG to IgM possible?","Is a switch back from IgG to IgM possible?","No"],["","In heavy chain recombination, what does the C region refer to?","In heavy chain recombination, what does the C region refer to?","Class switch recombination"]]},{"id":37056,"t":"Somatic Recombination","a":["rag-1/2","rag1/rag2","rag","rag1 and rag2","rag-1 and rag-2","recombination activating genes","recombination activation enzymes"],"s":"Name the recombination activation enzymes needed for V(D)J recombination.","e":"Somatic recombination requires recombination activation enzymes, including RAG-1 and RAG-2, along with recombination signal sequences and epigenetic changes to DNA structure.","k":"RAG-1/2 drive V(D)J recombination.","rn":[["","What 3 things does somatic recombination require?","What 3 things does somatic recombination require?","Recombination signal sequences; Recombination activation enzymes (RAG-1/2); Epigenetic changes"]]},{"id":37057,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"A footnote defines epigenetic changes. Which statement fits that definition?","o":["They modify DNA structure without changing the nucleotide sequence","They change the order of the nucleotides in the antibody gene sequence","They add new genes from the pathogen to the B cell","They only occur in mitochondrial DNA","They permanently delete the constant region genes"],"e":"Epigenetic changes are modifications to the structure of DNA that do NOT affect the nucleotide sequence. In somatic recombination they include changes to methylation and acetylation (adding methyl or acetyl groups).","w":["Epigenetic changes do not alter the nucleotide sequence.","Pathogen genes are not added to B cells.","The lecture does not restrict them to mitochondrial DNA.","Epigenetic changes do not delete genes."],"k":"Epigenetic = DNA structure change without sequence change.","rn":[["","What are epigenetic changes?","What are epigenetic changes?","Changes to DNA structure; Do NOT affect nucleotide sequence"]]},{"id":37058,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"Besides antibodies and B cell receptors, which other receptor is assembled by somatic recombination according to the speaker notes?","o":["The T cell receptor","The Fc receptor on phagocytes","The complement receptor CR1","Toll-like receptors","The mast cell IgE receptor"],"e":"The speaker notes say somatic recombination occurs in the assembly of genes that encode B cell receptors, antibodies and T cell receptors, and plays an essential role in the immune system's ability to respond to a huge variety of antigens.","w":["Fc receptors are not described as assembled by somatic recombination.","CR1 is a phagocyte receptor in a figure, not described as recombined.","Toll-like receptors are germline-encoded PRRs.","Mast cell receptors are not described as recombined."],"k":"Somatic recombination builds BCRs, antibodies and TCRs.","rn":[["","Somatic recombination assembles genes for which 3 molecules?","Somatic recombination assembles genes for which 3 molecules?","B cell receptors; Antibodies; T cell receptors"]]},{"id":37059,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"In this figure, what has happened in the cell at label 2 compared with label 1?","o":["The variable region DNA has been rearranged so V, D and J segments are joined","The constant region has switched from IgM to IgG","The cell has secreted soluble antibody into the blood","The mRNA has been translated into a T cell receptor","The DNA has been lost from the nucleus"],"e":"Label 1 is the immature B cell with heavy chain genes (V<sub>H</sub>, D, J, C<sub>μ</sub>) and light chain genes (V<sub>L</sub>, J, κ(λ)) still separated in germline configuration. Label 2 is the differentiating B cell with rearranged variable region receptor DNA, where V<sub>H</sub>DJ and V<sub>L</sub>J are joined. Label 3 shows the spliced RNA for an IgM<sub>m</sub> monomer, displayed on the surface at label 4.","w":["Class switching is not shown at this step; C<sub>μ</sub> is retained.","The figure ends with surface IgM, not secretion.","The figure shows a B cell making IgM, not a T cell receptor.","DNA is rearranged, not lost."],"k":"Germline → rearranged V(D)J DNA → spliced mRNA → surface IgM.","f":"bc15","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of B cell gene rearrangement?","What are the 3 stages of B cell gene rearrangement?","Immature: germline DNA; Differentiating: rearranged DNA; Immunocompetent: mRNA, surface IgM"],["","Recombination of which segments makes the heavy chain variable region?","Recombination of which segments makes the heavy chain variable region?","V, D and J"],["","What are the steps from germline DNA to light chain mRNA (messenger RNA)?","What are the steps from germline DNA to light chain mRNA (messenger RNA)?","Germline DNA; B cell DNA: V joined to J; Primary RNA transcript; mRNA: V, J and C joined"]]},{"id":37060,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"What is the first form in which a newly made specific antibody appears after somatic recombination?","o":["As a membrane-bound B cell receptor","As secreted IgG in the blood","As IgE bound to mast cells","As secretory IgA in the gut","As a soluble Fab fragment"],"e":"After somatic recombination, the immunocompetent B cell's spliced RNA codes for an individual IgM monomer expressed on the surface (sIgM), displaying the unique antigen-binding site. The specific antibody initially appears as a B cell receptor.","w":["Secreted IgG appears later, after activation and class switching.","IgE on mast cells is not the first form.","Secretory IgA is a mucosal form, not the first.","The antibody first appears as a whole membrane-bound molecule."],"k":"New antibody first appears as a membrane-bound BCR (surface IgM).","rn":[["","What is the membrane-bound form of the antibody called?","What is the membrane-bound form of the antibody called?","B cell receptor (BCR)"],["","What does the B cell receptor resemble?","What does the B cell receptor resemble?","A membrane-bound antibody"]]},{"id":37061,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"The heavy chain germline region contains V segments (V1…Vn), D1–D23 and J<sub>H</sub>1–J<sub>H</sub>6. What does recombination between V, D and J produce?","o":["The sequence of the heavy chain variable region","The sequence of the heavy chain constant region used for class switching","The light chain constant region","The J chain that links IgM units","The secretory piece of IgA"],"e":"Recombination between the V, D and J gene segments produces the sequence of the heavy chain variable region, called the functional VDJ gene. The C region refers to class switch recombination. A rearrangement in one B cell produces the final DNA sequence encoding that B cell's specific antibody.","w":["The C region is used in class switch recombination, not made by VDJ joining.","The light chain uses V, J and C segments; VDJ joining forms the heavy chain variable region.","The J chain is a separate linking polypeptide, not the J gene segment.","The secretory piece is part of secretory IgA."],"k":"V + D + J → heavy chain variable region; C region → class switching.","rn":[["","Recombination of which segments makes the heavy chain variable region?","Recombination of which segments makes the heavy chain variable region?","V, D and J"],["","In heavy chain recombination, what does the C region refer to?","In heavy chain recombination, what does the C region refer to?","Class switch recombination"]]},{"id":37062,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"The lecturer compared V(D)J recombination to choosing outfits from a wardrobe of about 3 pairs of trousers, 23 tops and 6 pairs of shoes. What is the key point of this analogy?","o":["Combining one item from each set gives many combinations from a limited number of parts","Each outfit is worn only once and then thrown away, like each antibody being used only once before it is destroyed","Only the trousers matter, like only the V segment matters","The wardrobe is the same in every person, so every antibody is identical","New clothes are bought for every infection, like new genes from pathogens"],"e":"The wardrobe analogy: with about 3 trousers, 23 tops and 6 pairs of shoes or socks, choosing one of each daily gives many combinations from what you have. B cells similarly have a variety of V, D, J and C segments and recombine them, with enzymes at the DNA level, to make specific antibodies.","w":["The analogy is about combinations, not single use.","Each segment type contributes to the combination.","The point is that recombination gives different antibodies in different B cells.","B cells rearrange their own gene segments, not genes from pathogens."],"k":"Combining one of each V, D, J segment gives huge diversity from few parts."},{"id":37063,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"Put the light chain steps in order: (P) primary RNA transcript; (Q) germline DNA with V, J and C segments; (R) mRNA V–J–C; (S) B-cell DNA with one V joined to one J.","o":["Q → S → P → R","S → Q → P → R","Q → P → S → R","P → Q → S → R","Q → S → R → P"],"e":"The light chain starts as germline DNA with V, J and C segments. In the B cell, one V is joined to one J (B-cell DNA). This is transcribed into a primary RNA transcript, which is spliced to mRNA containing V–J–C.","w":["Germline DNA comes before rearranged B-cell DNA.","The DNA is rearranged before it is transcribed.","Transcription follows DNA rearrangement.","mRNA is produced from the primary transcript, not before it."],"k":"Light chain: germline DNA → V–J joined DNA → primary RNA → mRNA.","rn":[["","What are the steps from germline DNA to light chain mRNA (messenger RNA)?","What are the steps from germline DNA to light chain mRNA (messenger RNA)?","Germline DNA; B cell DNA: V joined to J; Primary RNA transcript; mRNA: V, J and C joined"]]},{"id":37064,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"In this figure, which label marks the segments used in class switch recombination?","o":["3","1","2","4","5"],"e":"The labels show: 1 heavy chain germline segments (V<sub>H</sub>, D1–D23 and J<sub>H</sub>1–6); 2 the functional VDJ gene after recombination; 3 the constant (C) segments, used in class switch recombination; 4 light chain germline DNA with no D; 5 B-cell DNA to primary RNA to mRNA for the light chain.","w":["1 marks the heavy chain germline V, D and J segments.","2 marks the functional VDJ gene.","4 marks the light chain germline DNA.","5 marks the light chain processing steps."],"k":"C segments of the heavy chain are used in class switch recombination.","f":"bc16","rn":[["","Which heavy chain C segment is used in the first antibody, IgM?","Which heavy chain C segment is used in the first antibody, IgM?","Cμ"],["","In heavy chain recombination, what does the C region refer to?","In heavy chain recombination, what does the C region refer to?","Class switch recombination"]]},{"id":37065,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"A bacterium coated with antibodies is rapidly engulfed by a phagocyte. Which receptors on the phagocyte recognise the antibodies?","o":["Fc receptors that bind the antibody constant region","B cell receptors that bind the bacterial epitope","Toll-like receptors that bind the antibody variable region","J chain receptors that bind IgM pentamers only","HEV receptors in the lymph node"],"e":"The constant region (Fc) defines the antibody class and is recognised by specific Fc receptors. Fc receptors are found on leukocytes including phagocytes and help them identify pathogens already tagged by antibodies and eliminate them through phagocytosis.","w":["B cell receptors are on B cells and bind antigen, not antibody Fc regions.","Toll-like receptors are PRRs for pathogen patterns, not antibody variable regions.","J chain receptors are not described; Fc receptors recognise the constant region.","HEVs are vessels in lymph nodes, not phagocyte receptors."],"k":"Phagocyte Fc receptors bind antibody Fc regions → phagocytosis.","rn":[["","What receptors recognise the Fc region?","What receptors recognise the Fc region?","Fc receptors"],["","What is opsonisation?","What is opsonisation?","Antibody coats the pathogen; Macrophage FcR (Fc receptor) binds it; Leads to phagocytosis"],["","What are the 2 regions of an antibody?","What are the 2 regions of an antibody?","Variable (V); Constant (C)"]]},{"id":37066,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Which statement about the Fc region is correct?","o":["It is the constant region, defines the class and is recognised by Fc receptors","It is the variable region at the arm tips that binds the epitope on the antigen","It is found only on IgM molecules","It is the hypervariable loop of the light chain","It is removed before antibodies are secreted"],"e":"The constant region, Fc, defines the antibody class (IgG, IgA, IgD, IgE or IgM). It is recognised by Fc receptors on leukocytes, including phagocytes, and on other body cells. The variable region binds the epitope.","w":["The variable region, not Fc, binds the epitope.","All classes have an Fc region.","Hypervariable regions are in the variable domains.","Secreted antibodies keep their Fc region."],"k":"Fc = constant region; sets class; bound by Fc receptors.","rn":[["","What determines the antibody class?","What determines the antibody class?","Heavy chain (constant region)"],["","What receptors recognise the Fc region?","What receptors recognise the Fc region?","Fc receptors"],["","What does the Fc region define?","What does the Fc region define?","The antibody class"]]},{"id":37067,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"In this figure, the red box highlights one receptor among several on the phagocyte membrane. Which other receptors are shown alongside it?","o":["CR1 (CD35), CR3 (CD11b/CD18), CR4 (CD11c/CD18), CRIg and SIGNR1","CD4, CD8, CD28 and CD40","Toll-like receptors 1 to 5 only, which recognise the PAMPs on the coated pathogen","IgM, IgD and IgE B cell receptors","ACE2, CR2 and the J chain"],"e":"The figure shows a phagocyte engulfing an antibody-coated pathogen, with a magnified membrane patch showing CR1 (CD35), CR3 (CD11b/CD18), CR4 (CD11c/CD18), CRIg, Fc receptors (boxed in red) and SIGNR1. Reading from the bottom up, the receptors grab the Fc ends of antibodies stuck to the pathogen and the cell engulfs it.","w":["These are lymphocyte markers, not the receptors in this figure.","The figure does not show Toll-like receptors.","B cell receptors are on B cells, not this phagocyte.","These are not the receptors shown."],"k":"Fc receptors sit among complement and other receptors on phagocytes.","f":"bc17","rn":[["","Which other receptors sit on phagocytes with Fc receptors?","Which other receptors sit on phagocytes with Fc receptors?","CR1 (CD35); CR3 (CD11b/CD18); CR4 (CD11c/CD18); CRIg; SIGNR1"]]},{"id":37068,"t":"Somatic Recombination","a":["fc receptor","fc receptors","fcr","fc gamma receptor"],"s":"Name the receptors on phagocytes that recognise the constant region of antibodies bound to a pathogen.","e":"Fc receptors recognise the Fc (constant) region of antibodies. On phagocytes they help identify pathogens tagged by antibodies and eliminate them by phagocytosis.","k":"Fc receptors bind antibody constant regions.","rn":[["","What receptors recognise the Fc region?","What receptors recognise the Fc region?","Fc receptors"],["","What is the job of Fc receptors on phagocytes?","What is the job of Fc receptors on phagocytes?","Identify antibody-bound pathogens; Eliminate them by phagocytosis"]]},{"id":37069,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Put the stages of B cell development in order: (P) negative selection removes self-reactive cells; (Q) progenitors emerge and differentiate in the bone marrow; (R) maturation to plasma cells in the spleen; (S) somatic recombination alters each cell's DNA; (T) immature B cells enter the blood.","o":["Q → S → P → T → R","Q → P → S → T → R","S → Q → P → R → T","Q → S → T → P → R","Q → T → S → P → R"],"e":"B cell progenitors emerge in the bone marrow and differentiate. There they undergo somatic (V(D)J) recombination, and negative selection eliminates B cells whose antibodies react with self antigens. Committed but still immature B cells enter the blood, and in the spleen they meet antigens and complete maturation to plasma cells.","w":["Recombination must create receptors before they can be tested in negative selection.","Progenitors emerge first, and maturation in the spleen comes last.","Negative selection happens in the bone marrow before release into the blood.","Recombination and selection happen before the cells enter the blood."],"k":"Marrow: emerge → V(D)J → negative selection → blood → spleen maturation.","rn":[["","Negative Selection","Negative Selection","B cells reacting with self-antigens are eliminated; In the bone marrow"],["","Which 2 processes happen to B cells in the bone marrow?","Which 2 processes happen to B cells in the bone marrow?","Somatic (V(D)J) recombination; Negative selection"]]},{"id":37070,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"In the bone marrow, a developing B cell's antibody binds strongly to an endogenous antigen. What happens to it?","o":["It is eliminated by negative selection","It is released into the blood as a memory cell","It undergoes affinity maturation to bind better","It switches class to IgE","It moves to the thymus for retraining"],"e":"In the bone marrow, negative selection eliminates B cells whose antibodies react with endogenous (self) antigens. Those that do not react well with self antigens enter the blood circulation as immature B cells.","w":["Self-reactive cells are eliminated, not released as memory cells.","Affinity maturation follows antigen encounter in mature B cells, not self-reactivity.","Class switching to IgE is not the fate of self-reactive cells.","B cells do not go to the thymus."],"k":"Negative selection removes self-reactive B cells in the marrow.","rn":[["","Negative Selection","Negative Selection","B cells reacting with self-antigens are eliminated; In the bone marrow"]]},{"id":37071,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"The lecturer calls the spleen a \"B cell school\". What does this mean?","o":["Immature B cells specialise there after meeting antigen and mature into plasma cells","B cells are first born there from haematopoietic stem cells before moving to the marrow","B cells undergo somatic recombination there","Self-reactive B cells are removed there by negative selection","T cells train B cells there to switch back from IgG to IgM"],"e":"The spleen is where immature B cells come into contact with antigens and complete their maturation to plasma cells. The lecturer described this as a B cell school: B cells become specialists, identify the specific antigen that cross-links well with their BCR, and make more antibody against it.","w":["Progenitors emerge in the bone marrow.","Somatic recombination occurs in the bone marrow.","Negative selection occurs in the bone marrow.","Switching back from IgG to IgM is not possible."],"k":"Spleen = B cell school: maturation to plasma cells after antigen contact.","rn":[["","What do B cells mature into in the spleen?","What do B cells mature into in the spleen?","Plasma cells"],["","What happens when an immature B cell meets antigen that cross-links its BCR (B cell rec…","What happens when an immature B cell meets antigen that cross-links its BCR (B cell receptor) well?","It proliferates and survives; Becomes a reservoir for antibody against that antigen"]]},{"id":37072,"t":"Antibody Structure","s":"Which antibody class is mainly produced first by developing B cells, and which do they produce as they mature?","o":["Mainly IgM first; then other classes such as IgG","Mainly IgG first, then IgM once the B cell has met its antigen in the spleen","Mainly IgE first; then IgA","Mainly IgA first; then IgD","Only IgD throughout development"],"e":"The antibody initially produced by B cells in the bone marrow is primarily IgM (the figure legend shows IgM and IgD). As B cells mature they start producing other classes, such as IgG.","w":["IgM comes first; switching from IgG back to IgM is not possible.","IgE and IgA are not the first antibodies made.","IgA is not the first class.","B cells progress from IgM to other classes."],"k":"Developing B cells make mainly IgM first, then other classes such as IgG.","rn":[["","What are the 5 antibody classes?","What are the 5 antibody classes?","IgG; IgA; IgM; IgD; IgE; MNEMONIC"],["","Which 3 antibody classes are monomers?","Which 3 antibody classes are monomers?","IgG; IgD; IgE"]]},{"id":37073,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"In this figure, which label marks where B cells complete their maturation?","o":["5","1","2","4","6"],"e":"The labels show: 1 somatic (V(D)J) recombination in the bone marrow; 2 negative selection; 3 endogenous (self) antigen; 4 immature B cell leaving via the central sinus; 5 the spleen, where maturation is completed; 6 a mature B cell; 7 legend for IgM and IgD.","w":["1 marks somatic recombination in the bone marrow.","2 marks negative selection.","4 marks the immature B cell leaving the marrow.","6 marks the mature B cell, not the site of maturation."],"k":"B cells complete maturation in the spleen.","f":"bc19","rn":[["","Where do immature B cells complete maturation?","Where do immature B cells complete maturation?","Spleen"]]},{"id":37074,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Which statement about the B cell receptor is correct?","o":["It works with co-receptors and receptor-associated adapter molecules","It signals entirely on its own, without needing any co-receptor or adapter molecule","It is secreted as soon as the B cell is made","It binds antigen only after class switching to IgE","It is a T cell receptor borrowed by the B cell"],"e":"The B cell receptor resembles a membrane-bound antibody and is the first antibody made after V(D)J recombination. It does not work on its own: it cooperates with co-receptor molecules and receptor-associated (adapter) molecules.","w":["The BCR needs co-receptors and adapters.","The BCR is membrane-bound on naive cells.","The BCR binds antigen from the start.","The BCR is a membrane-bound antibody, not a T cell receptor."],"k":"BCR = membrane antibody working with co-receptors and adapters.","rn":[["","What 2 types of molecule does the BCR cooperate with?","What 2 types of molecule does the BCR cooperate with?","Co-receptor molecules; Receptor-associated molecules"]]},{"id":37075,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Put the BCR signalling steps in order: (P) altered gene expression; (Q) phosphorylation of associated molecules; (R) antigen binds the BCR; (S) signalling cascade; (T) cell proliferation and survival.","o":["R → Q → S → P → T","Q → R → S → P → T","R → S → Q → P → T","R → Q → P → S → T","P → R → Q → S → T"],"e":"Antigen binding is followed by a phosphorylation step of the associated molecules that initiates a signalling cascade (signal transduction), leading to altered gene expression and so to changes in cell proliferation and survival.","w":["Antigen must bind before phosphorylation occurs.","Phosphorylation initiates the cascade.","Gene expression changes come after the cascade.","Gene expression is near the end, not the start."],"k":"Antigen → phosphorylation → cascade → gene expression → proliferation/survival.","rn":[["","BCR signalling cascade alters gene expression to change which 2 things?","BCR signalling cascade alters gene expression to change which 2 things?","Cell proliferation; Cell survival"]]},{"id":37076,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Immature B cells circulating in the blood are \"not meant to be around for long\". Why does one particular cell survive and multiply?","o":["It meets an antigen that cross-links well with its BCR, triggering survival signals","It is the oldest cell in the circulation","It lacks a BCR and so avoids negative selection","It has already switched to IgG before meeting its antigen, so it binds more strongly","It is selected because it binds self antigens strongly"],"e":"Immature B cells circulate in search of antigen. When one meets an antigen that cross-links well with its BCR, the signalling cascade promotes that cell's proliferation and survival, because it has become a reservoir of information for producing antibody against that antigen.","w":["Survival depends on antigen binding, not age.","B cells need a BCR to receive these signals.","Class switching follows activation.","Self-reactive cells are eliminated."],"k":"Good antigen cross-linking of the BCR → survival and proliferation.","rn":[["","What happens when an immature B cell meets antigen that cross-links its BCR (B cell rec…","What happens when an immature B cell meets antigen that cross-links its BCR (B cell receptor) well?","It proliferates and survives; Becomes a reservoir for antibody against that antigen"]]},{"id":37077,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"In this figure, what is shown at label 4?","o":["Kinases and adapters (Lyn, Syk, BLNK) being phosphorylated","The antigen cross-linking two BCRs","The Igα/Igβ receptor-associated molecules","The final outcome of cell proliferation and survival","The BCR with no antigen bound"],"e":"The figure labels: 1 the BCR; 2 the Igα/Igβ receptor-associated molecules; 3 antigen cross-linking two BCRs; 4 kinases and adapters (Lyn, Syk, BLNK) being phosphorylated; 5 signal transduction and receptor internalisation; 6 cell proliferation and survival. You do not need the individual molecules for this session.","w":["Antigen cross-linking is at label 3.","Igα/Igβ is at label 2.","The outcome is at label 6.","The BCR alone is at label 1."],"k":"BCR signalling: antigen cross-link → phosphorylation of kinases/adapters → cascade.","f":"bc20","rn":[["","Which molecules signal with the BCR?","Which molecules signal with the BCR?","Igα/Igβ; Lyn; Syk; BLNK"]]},{"id":37078,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"Haemopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow rearrange their genes so each B cell has a slightly different BCR. Which statement about this step is correct?","o":["It happens independently of antigen","It happens only after antigen is met","It produces identical BCRs in every B cell","It takes place in the spleen","It is the same as affinity maturation"],"e":"Gene rearrangements in the bone marrow give each B cell a slightly different BCR. This process is independent of antigen presence, and because millions of different B cells emerge, there is capacity to recognise large numbers of antigens. Once rearrangement is complete the B cell is antigenically committed.","w":["Rearrangement happens before any antigen is met.","Each B cell has a slightly different BCR.","Rearrangement occurs in the bone marrow.","Affinity maturation happens later, after first antigen encounter."],"k":"Gene rearrangement is antigen-independent.","rn":[["","Do an antibody's two binding domains work together or alone?","Do an antibody's two binding domains work together or alone?","Each binds antigen independently; Each has its own on and off rates"],["","What happens right after antigen binds the BCR?","What happens right after antigen binds the BCR?","Phosphorylation step"],["","What happens to IgM and IgG levels once antigen is neutralised?","What happens to IgM and IgG levels once antigen is neutralised?","They fall over time"]]},{"id":37079,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts plasma cells and memory B cells?","o":["Plasma cells are short-lived antibody factories; memory cells live for decades","Plasma cells live for decades in tissues; memory cells die within days of the infection","Both live for decades and secrete antibody constantly","Plasma cells carry the BCR only; memory cells secrete IgE only","Both are made only in the thymus"],"e":"A B cell that binds antigen divides into identical clones, plasma cells, which are factories for antibody production but live for a short time. It can also differentiate into memory cells with a very long lifespan; they can reside in tissues for decades and respond very fast to a repeat infection.","w":["This reverses the lifespans.","Plasma cells are short-lived.","Neither statement is described.","B cells mature in the bone marrow and spleen, not the thymus."],"k":"Plasma cells: short-lived factories; memory cells: decades.","rn":[["","Plasma Cells","Plasma Cells","Identical daughter clones; Antibody factories; Short lifespan"]]},{"id":37080,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"After first meeting antigen, a mature B cell makes minor changes to the amino acid sequence of its antibody's variable domain, improving binding. What is this process?","o":["Affinity maturation","Somatic recombination in the bone marrow","Class switching of the constant region","Negative selection of self-reactive cells","Clonal expansion into plasma cells"],"e":"Affinity maturation involves minor changes in the amino acid sequence of the variable domain in the mature B cell once it encounters antigen for the first time, further improving antigen-binding affinity. Somatic recombination happens earlier and independently of antigen; class switching changes the constant region.","w":["Somatic recombination is antigen-independent gene rearrangement in the marrow.","Class switching changes the constant region, not the variable domain.","Negative selection removes self-reactive cells.","Clonal expansion is division into identical clones."],"k":"Affinity maturation: small variable-domain changes after first antigen meeting.","rn":[["","Affinity Maturation","Affinity Maturation","Minor amino acid changes in variable domain; After first antigen encounter"]]},{"id":37081,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","a":["affinity maturation"],"s":"Name the process of small amino acid changes in the variable domain, after first antigen encounter, that strengthen antibody binding.","e":"Affinity maturation involves minor changes in the amino acid sequence of the variable domain in the mature B cell once it first encounters antigen, making the interaction stronger.","k":"Affinity maturation improves antibody affinity.","rn":[["","Affinity Maturation","Affinity Maturation","Minor amino acid changes in variable domain; After first antigen encounter"]]},{"id":37082,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"In this figure, why does only cell 2 go on to produce plasma cells and memory cells?","o":["Only cell 2 has a receptor that binds antigen 2 on the target pathogen","Cell 2 is the only one of the four that underwent gene rearrangement in the marrow","Cell 2 is the only cell that left the bone marrow","The other cells were converted into T cells","Cell 2 is the oldest stem cell"],"e":"Gene rearrangement gives cells with different receptors (1–4). Only B cell number 2 expresses a receptor that binds antigen on the target pathogen. Binding leads to antigen-dependent proliferation and differentiation in peripheral lymphoid tissue into plasma cells secreting antibody 2 and long-lived memory cells.","w":["All the cells rearranged their genes; that is why they differ.","All mature cells leave the marrow; only cell 2 binds the antigen.","B cells do not become T cells.","Selection depends on antigen binding, not age."],"k":"Only the B cell whose receptor fits the antigen expands.","f":"bc21","rn":[["","What happens in peripheral lymphoid tissue during B cell maturation?","What happens in peripheral lymphoid tissue during B cell maturation?","Antigen-dependent proliferation; Differentiation into plasma and memory cells"],["","Why does only one B cell respond to a given antigen?","Why does only one B cell respond to a given antigen?","Only it has a receptor that binds that antigen; Other B cells have different receptors"],["","What 2 cell types does an antigen-binding B cell become?","What 2 cell types does an antigen-binding B cell become?","Plasma cells; Memory cells"]]},{"id":37083,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"What are the two binding events required to activate a B cell for clonal expansion?","o":["Antigen binding to its receptor and stimulation by growth factors such as interleukin-2","Complement binding and phagocytosis by a macrophage","Fc receptor binding on the B cell together with production of IgE by mast cells","Binding of two different antigens to two receptors","Self-antigen binding and negative selection"],"e":"Each B cell (and T cell) expresses its own antigen receptor. Activation requires not only binding of antigen to the receptor but also stimulation by growth factors such as interleukin-2. Cytokines take part, as covered further in the Musculoskeletal and Immune Systems module.","w":["Complement and phagocytosis are not the activating signals described.","Fc receptors are on phagocytes; IgE is not an activation requirement.","One specific antigen plus a growth factor is needed.","Self-antigen binding leads to elimination."],"k":"Activation = antigen on receptor + growth factor (e.g. IL-2).","rn":[["","What 2 signals does B cell activation require?","What 2 signals does B cell activation require?","Antigen binding to receptor; Growth factors (e.g. interleukin-2)"]]},{"id":37084,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"A B cell's receptor binds antigen, but the cell receives no growth factor signal. What is the likely outcome?","o":["Anergy, an inability to be activated later, or death by apoptosis","Normal clonal expansion into plasma cells","Immediate class switching to IgG, with the cell going on to divide normally anyway","Conversion into a memory T cell","Migration to the thymus"],"e":"Either binding event alone (antigen binding or growth factor stimulation) can result in an inability to subsequently be activated (anergy), or even cell death by apoptosis. Clonal expansion needs both signals.","w":["Clonal expansion requires both signals.","Class switching follows full activation.","B cells do not become T cells.","B cells do not migrate to the thymus."],"k":"One signal alone → anergy or apoptosis.","rn":[["","Anergy","Anergy","Inability to be activated later"]]},{"id":37085,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","a":["anergy"],"s":"What is the term for the inability of a lymphocyte to be activated later, after it received only one of the two activating signals?","e":"Anergy is an inability to be subsequently activated. It can follow when a lymphocyte receives only one of the two required binding events, antigen or growth factor; the cell may even die by apoptosis.","k":"Anergy: unresponsiveness after an incomplete activation signal.","rn":[["","Anergy","Anergy","Inability to be activated later"],["","What happens if only one activation signal is received?","What happens if only one activation signal is received?","Anergy; Or apoptosis"]]},{"id":37086,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Which statement about clonal expansion is correct?","o":["Activation of one cell produces clones with the same antigen-binding capability","It produces clones that each bind a different antigen, widening the response to the pathogen","It happens only in T cells, never in B cells","It requires only antigen binding, with no other signal","It produces only memory cells and no effector cells"],"e":"Each B cell (and T cell) has its own antigen receptor. Activation of a particular cell leads to clones with the same antigen-binding capability. The figure shows differentiation into effector cells and memory cells, both antigen specific; memory cells give the secondary response.","w":["Clones share the same antigen specificity.","Both B and T cells undergo clonal expansion.","Growth factor stimulation is also required.","Effector cells and memory cells are both produced."],"k":"Clonal expansion: identical clones with the same specificity.","rn":[["","Clonal Expansion","Clonal Expansion","Activated cell makes clones; Same antigen binding capability"]]},{"id":37087,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"In this figure, which outcome follows from the memory cells at label 6?","o":["The secondary response","B-cell antibody synthesis in the primary response only","Negative selection in the bone marrow","Selective activation of a virgin lymphocyte","Somatic recombination of V, D and J"],"e":"The clonal expansion figure shows selective activation of a virgin lymphocyte by antigen, clonal proliferation and differentiation into effector cells (B-cell antibody synthesis, T-cell help, regulation, cytotoxicity and cytokine release) and memory cells, which provide the secondary response. Both are antigen specific.","w":["Antibody synthesis is listed under effector cells.","Negative selection is not shown in this figure.","Selective activation is the starting step at the top.","Somatic recombination is not part of this figure."],"k":"Memory cells from clonal expansion give the secondary response.","f":"bc22","rn":[["","What do memory cells give after clonal expansion?","What do memory cells give after clonal expansion?","An antigen-specific secondary response"]]},{"id":37088,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"Which pairing of process and location is correct?","o":["Somatic recombination: bone marrow; completion of maturation: spleen","Somatic recombination: spleen; negative selection of self-reactive B cells: thymus","Negative selection: spleen; maturation: bone marrow","Class switching: bone marrow before antigen is met","Affinity maturation: bone marrow before antigen is met"],"e":"B cell progenitors emerge in the bone marrow, undergo somatic recombination and negative selection there, and enter the blood as immature B cells. In the spleen they meet antigen and complete maturation. Affinity maturation and class switching happen after antigen is met.","w":["Recombination is in the marrow, and B cells are not selected in the thymus.","Negative selection is in the marrow; maturation is completed in the spleen.","Class switching follows antigen encounter.","Affinity maturation follows first antigen encounter."],"k":"Marrow: recombination + negative selection; spleen: maturation.","rn":[["","Which 2 processes happen to B cells in the bone marrow?","Which 2 processes happen to B cells in the bone marrow?","Somatic (V(D)J) recombination; Negative selection"]]},{"id":37089,"t":"What Is an Antibody","s":"Which of the following is antigen-INDEPENDENT?","o":["Gene rearrangement giving each B cell a different BCR","Affinity maturation of the variable domain","Clonal expansion into plasma cells","Class switching from IgM to IgG","Production of memory cells"],"e":"The speaker notes state that gene rearrangement in the bone marrow, giving each B cell a slightly different BCR, is independent of antigen presence. Affinity maturation, clonal expansion, class switching and memory cell production all follow antigen encounter.","w":["Affinity maturation follows first antigen encounter.","Clonal expansion follows antigen binding.","Class switching happens during the immune response to antigen.","Memory cells form after antigen binding."],"k":"Gene rearrangement is antigen-independent; later steps need antigen.","rn":[["","What happens after the BCR is activated?","What happens after the BCR is activated?","BCR gene rearrangements; Secretion of soluble antibody"],["","What happens in the bone marrow during B cell maturation?","What happens in the bone marrow during B cell maturation?","Gene rearrangement; Maturation into mature antigenetically committed B cells"],["","After gene rearrangement, the B cell is now what?","After gene rearrangement, the B cell is now what?","Antigenically committed"]]},{"id":37090,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A B cell has been activated by antigen and divides. Which outcome describes the lifespan of its products correctly?","o":["Plasma cells make antibody briefly; memory cells persist and react fast later","Plasma cells persist for decades; memory cells make antibody briefly","All clones become long-lived plasma cells","All clones become T helper cells","The clones revert to naive B cells after infection"],"e":"An antigen-binding B cell divides into identical daughter clones, plasma cells, which are factories for antibody production but live for a short period. It can also differentiate into memory cells with a very long lifespan, residing in tissues for decades and reacting quickly if the antigen returns.","w":["The lifespans are reversed.","Plasma cells are short-lived.","B cells do not become T cells.","Memory cells persist; they do not revert to naive cells."],"k":"Activated B cell → short-lived plasma cells + long-lived memory cells.","rn":[["","What makes the secondary response fast?","What makes the secondary response fast?","Memory B cells; Quickly produce plasma cells"]]},{"id":37091,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A toxin-producing infection is treated with antibodies that bind the toxin before it reaches host cells. Which antibody function is this?","o":["Neutralisation, preventing pathogen–host binding","Opsonisation via macrophage Fc receptors, leading to phagocytosis of the toxin","Complement fixation and formation of the MAC","Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity","Eosinophil degranulation on a helminth"],"e":"Antibodies can neutralise viruses and toxins: they bind toxin molecules or pathogens and prevent pathogen–host binding. Other roles are opsonisation (Fc receptors on macrophages → phagocytosis), complement fixation with the membrane attack complex (phagocytosis or lysis), ADCC by NK cells and eosinophil degranulation.","w":["Opsonisation leads to phagocytosis rather than blocking toxin binding.","Complement fixation leads to phagocytosis or lysis.","ADCC uses NK cells to kill infected or tumour cells.","Degranulation by eosinophils targets parasites."],"k":"Neutralisation blocks pathogen or toxin binding to host cells.","rn":[["","How does neutralisation protect against viruses and toxins?","How does neutralisation protect against viruses and toxins?","Antibody binds the virus or toxin; Prevents pathogen-host binding"]]},{"id":37092,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A tumour cell coated with antibodies is killed when a natural killer cell binds the antibodies and triggers apoptosis. Which mechanism is this?","o":["Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC)","Neutralisation of viruses and toxins","Opsonisation of the tumour cell by macrophages carrying Fc receptors","Agglutination into clumps","Secretory IgA transport"],"e":"In antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC), NK cells bind antibody on infected or tumour cells, which leads to NK-induced apoptosis. Antibodies can be involved in tumour cell recognition and NK cell activity.","w":["Neutralisation blocks binding of pathogens or toxins.","Opsonisation leads to phagocytosis by macrophages.","Agglutination clumps antigens together.","Secretory IgA crosses epithelium into the lumen."],"k":"ADCC: NK cells kill antibody-coated cells by apoptosis.","rn":[["","How does ADCC (antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity) work?","How does ADCC (antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity) work?","Antibodies coat an infected or tumour cell; NK (natural killer) cell binds them; NK-induced apoptosis"],["","What does ADCC stand for?","What does ADCC stand for?","Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity"]]},{"id":37093,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Why does agglutination help clear bacteria from the gut or airways?","o":["Cross-linked clumps get trapped in mucus and are cleared out","Clumps make bacteria smaller so they pass into the blood","Clumps convert bacteria into commensals","Clumps release histamine from the bacteria, which expels them from the lumen","Clumps let bacteria enter cells more easily"],"e":"Antibodies have several antigen sites per molecule (2 per IgG, 4 per IgA, 10 per IgM), so one antibody can bind and cross-link multiple antigens, causing agglutination. Clumps impair the pathogen, reduce its ability to enter cells and help clearance: in the intestine or respiratory tract, agglutinated bacteria are trapped in mucus and cleared.","w":["Clumping makes bacteria bigger aggregates, not smaller.","Agglutination does not make bacteria commensal.","Histamine is from mast cells, not clumps.","Agglutination reduces the ability to enter cells."],"k":"Agglutination: clumps trapped in mucus and cleared.","rn":[["","Where are agglutinated bacteria trapped and cleared?","Where are agglutinated bacteria trapped and cleared?","Mucus; Intestine, respiratory tract"],["","What is agglutination?","What is agglutination?","Antibodies cross-link antigens; Form antigen-antibody clumps"]]},{"id":37094,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"How many antigen sites per molecule do IgG, IgA and IgM have, according to the speaker notes on agglutination?","o":["IgG 2, IgA 4, IgM 10","IgG 1, IgA 2, IgM 5","IgG 2, IgA 2, IgM 2","IgG 4, IgA 10, IgM 2","IgG 10, IgA 4, IgM 2"],"e":"Antibodies have multiple antigen sites per molecule: 2 per IgG, 4 per IgA and 10 per IgM. Multiple sites let one antibody cross-link several antigens, causing agglutination and neutralisation. The values 1, 2 and 5 are the valency figures (counted in four-chain units) in the properties table.","w":["These are the valency values (four-chain units), not binding sites.","IgA and IgM have more than two sites.","The numbers are reversed.","The numbers are reversed."],"k":"Binding sites: IgG 2, IgA 4, IgM 10.","rn":[["","How many antigen sites per molecule: IgG vs IgA vs IgM?","How many antigen sites per molecule: IgG vs IgA vs IgM?","IgG 2; IgA 4; IgM 10"]]},{"id":37095,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"An eosinophil attacks a helminth by degranulation. Which antibody–receptor pairing is labelled for this in the figure?","o":["FcεR and IgE","Fc receptor and IgM","J chain and IgA","Complement C1q and IgD","Poly-Ig receptor and IgG"],"e":"The antibodies-and-immunity figure shows eosinophil degranulation on a helminth labelled \"(FcεR and IgE)\". Eosinophils have Fc receptors and can recognise parasites through antibody. IgE binds mast cells and basophils and initiates inflammation in parasitic infections.","w":["IgM is not the antibody labelled for eosinophil degranulation.","The J chain links IgA units.","C1q is part of complement fixation.","The poly-Ig receptor transports IgA across epithelium."],"k":"Eosinophil degranulation on helminths: FcεR + IgE.","rn":[["","How does degranulation attack helminths?","How does degranulation attack helminths?","Antibody IgE coats the helminth; Eosinophil FcεR (Fc epsilon receptor) binds IgE; Eosinophil degranulates"]]},{"id":37096,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"In this figure, what does label 9 show?","o":["Secreted IgA crossing gut epithelium into the lumen","A plasma cell secreting antibody directly into the bloodstream","An NK cell killing a tumour cell","Complement forming the MAC on a tumour cell","A macrophage engulfing an opsonised bacterium"],"e":"The figure labels: 1 plasma cell; 2 the antibody with variable (Fab) and constant (Fc) regions; 3 neutralisation; 4 opsonisation by macrophage FcR; 5 complement fixation and MAC; 6 ADCC by an NK cell; 7 eosinophil degranulation on a helminth; 8 the endothelial cell endosome; 9 secreted IgA crossing gut epithelium via the poly-Ig receptor into the GI lumen.","w":["The plasma cell is at label 1.","ADCC is at label 6.","Complement fixation is at label 5.","Opsonisation is at label 4."],"k":"Secretory IgA crosses epithelium into the lumen to protect against antigens.","f":"bc24","rn":[["","Where is IgA secreted to protect against antigens?","Where is IgA secreted to protect against antigens?","Lumen of GI tract"]]},{"id":37097,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Which statement about Fc receptors on endothelial cells is shown in the antibodies-and-immunity figure?","o":["They take antibody into an acidic endosome and release it back at physiological pH","They destroy the antibodies permanently inside lysosomes of the endothelium","They convert IgG into IgA for secretion","They make antibodies cross the placenta as IgM","They block antibodies from entering the blood"],"e":"The figure shows Fc receptors on an endothelial cell taking antibody into an acidic endosome, where it is protected, and releasing it by dissociation at physiological pH back into the blood. The lecturer noted that Fc receptors can be present on endothelial cells for recognition of antibodies.","w":["The antibody is released back to the blood, not destroyed.","Endothelial cells do not change antibody class.","Only IgG crosses the placenta.","The antibody is returned to the blood."],"k":"Endothelial Fc receptors recycle antibody back into blood.","f":"bc24","rn":[["","How do endothelial cells recycle IgG?","How do endothelial cells recycle IgG?","Binds receptor in acidic endosome; Released at physiological pH"]]},{"id":37098,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","a":["agglutination"],"s":"What term describes clumping of antigens that are cross-linked by antibodies?","e":"Agglutination is the formation of antigen–antibody clumps. Because antibodies have multiple sites (2 per IgG, 4 per IgA, 10 per IgM), they cross-link antigens, impairing pathogens and aiding clearance in mucus.","k":"Agglutination = clumping by cross-linking antibodies.","rn":[["","What is agglutination?","What is agglutination?","Antibodies cross-link antigens; Form antigen-antibody clumps"]]},{"id":37099,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","a":["opsonisation","opsonization"],"s":"Name the process in which antibody coating lets macrophages recognise a pathogen through Fc receptors and phagocytose it.","e":"Opsonisation: macrophage Fc receptors bind antibody-coated pathogens, leading to phagocytosis. It is one of the roles shown in the antibodies-and-immunity figure.","k":"Opsonisation: antibody coating → FcR binding → phagocytosis.","rn":[["","What is opsonisation?","What is opsonisation?","Antibody coats the pathogen; Macrophage FcR (Fc receptor) binds it; Leads to phagocytosis"]]},{"id":37100,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A person meets a new antigen for the first time. Which sequence of antibody production fits the primary response?","o":["Lag, then IgD and IgM, then IgG after class switching","Immediate IgG with no lag at all, followed later by IgM once the antigen is cleared","Lag, then IgE, then IgA","IgG first, then switching back to IgM","Only IgD throughout, with no switching"],"e":"After first exposure there is a lag period of 4–7 days before the first response begins with production of IgD and then IgM antibodies specific for the antigen. In time, class switching and affinity maturation lead to production of IgG, the main mature antibody form.","w":["There is a lag, and IgM comes before IgG.","IgE and IgA are not the primary sequence described.","Switching back from IgG to IgM is not possible.","The response switches to IgG."],"k":"Primary: lag → IgD/IgM → class switch → IgG.","rn":[["","What makes IgG appear in the primary response?","What makes IgG appear in the primary response?","Class switching; Affinity maturation"]]},{"id":37101,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"What does the lag phase of the primary response represent?","o":["The time for the pathogen to propagate and for circulating B cells to find the antigen","The time needed for memory B cells from an earlier infection to make plasma cells","The time for antibodies to switch back from IgG to IgM","The time for the thymus to mature B cells","The time for IgE to bind mast cells"],"e":"The lag phase runs from infection to the production of antibodies. It describes the time it takes for the pathogen to start propagating and for circulating B cells to identify and find the antigen. The speaker notes give 4–7 days.","w":["Memory B cells act in the secondary response.","Switching back to IgM is not possible.","B cells mature in the bone marrow and spleen, not the thymus.","IgE and mast cells are not the explanation for the lag."],"k":"Lag = time for pathogen to spread and B cells to find antigen.","rn":[["","What happens during the lag phase?","What happens during the lag phase?","Pathogen starts to multiply; Circulating B cells find the antigen"]]},{"id":37102,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Early IgM in the primary response may have less than optimal affinity. Why is it still effective?","o":["Its pentameric structure gives high functional affinity (avidity)","It binds covalently to the antigen, so the complex can never fall apart","It crosses the placenta into the fetus","It binds mast cells to hold the antigen","It is converted into IgG instantly"],"e":"At the start of the primary response, the affinity of the binding site for the epitope may be less than optimal, but the pentameric structure of IgM leads to a high functional affinity (avidity), like Velcro, where many weak bonds add up to a strong hold.","w":["Antibody binding is reversible, not covalent.","Only IgG crosses the placenta.","Binding mast cells is an IgE property.","Class switching takes time; it is not instant."],"k":"IgM's pentamer gives high avidity despite lower early affinity.","rn":[["","Primary response: why does early IgM bind well despite low affinity?","Primary response: why does early IgM bind well despite low affinity?","Pentamer gives high avidity"]]},{"id":37103,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A B cell switches from producing IgM to IgG during an infection. Which statement is correct?","o":["The constant domain changes; the antigen specificity stays the same","The variable domain changes during switching, so a new antigen is recognised","The cell can later switch back to IgM if needed","Both domains change completely","The B cell becomes a T cell"],"e":"Class switching affects the constant domain, so the antibody retains affinity for the same antigen but can interact with different effector molecules and be recognised by different receptors. A switch back from IgG to IgM is not possible. IgM is more energy-consuming, so switching to IgG is more efficient.","w":["The variable domain is unchanged, so specificity is kept.","Switching back from IgG to IgM is not possible.","Only the constant domain changes.","B cells do not become T cells."],"k":"Class switch: new constant domain, same specificity, no going back.","rn":[["","Class Switching","Class Switching","Changes the constant domain; Same antigen affinity, different effector molecules"]]},{"id":37104,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Why does it make sense for B cells to switch from IgM to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","o":["IgM is more energy-consuming, so switching to the more efficient IgG saves energy","IgM cannot bind antigen at all","IgG binds a different and more important antigen on the same pathogen","IgM is destroyed by the spleen","IgG is the only antibody with a variable region"],"e":"The lecturer noted that IgM is more energy-consuming to produce, so it makes sense for cells to switch to producing the more energy-efficient IgG. The speaker notes also say IgM is energetically expensive for B cells. Switching keeps the same specificity because only the constant domain changes.","w":["IgM binds antigen well, with high avidity.","Switching keeps the same antigen.","The spleen is not described as destroying IgM.","All antibodies have variable regions."],"k":"IgM is costly; IgG is more energy-efficient.","rn":[["","Why do B cells switch to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","Why do B cells switch to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","IgM uses more energy; IgG more energy efficient"]]},{"id":37105,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"On a second exposure to the same antigen, the response is faster. What is the main reason?","o":["Memory B cells produced after the first exposure quickly make plasma cells","Naive B cells divide faster the second time","IgM levels from the first infection stay at peak","The lag period is the same as before, but much more IgE is made","Innate immunity has learned the antigen"],"e":"The speed of the secondary response depends on the memory B cells produced after the first exposure. On the second exposure memory B cells can produce plasma cells quickly, and the bulk of antibody made is IgG.","w":["The responding cells are memory B cells, not naive cells.","IgM and IgG fall after the antigen is neutralised.","The lag is shorter and IgG predominates.","Innate immunity has no memory."],"k":"Memory B cells → quick plasma cells → fast IgG secondary response.","rn":[["","What makes the secondary response fast?","What makes the secondary response fast?","Memory B cells; Quickly produce plasma cells"]]},{"id":37106,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"After a second exposure has cleared the antigen, what happens to antibody and memory cells?","o":["IgG may or may not fall; memory B cells remain for long periods, even a lifetime","All antibody and all memory cells disappear within a few weeks of clearance","IgM rises steadily for life","Memory cells switch back to IgM production","Plasma cells live for the rest of life"],"e":"When the antigen has been removed, antigen-specific IgG in the blood may or may not decrease, and memory B cells remain for long periods, even an entire lifetime. So re-exposure at any point can immediately start a response equal in speed and magnitude to the initial secondary response.","w":["Memory cells persist.","IgM does not rise for life.","Switching back to IgM is not possible.","Plasma cells are short-lived; memory cells persist."],"k":"Memory B cells persist, possibly for life.","rn":[["","Memory Cells","Memory Cells","Very long lifespan (decades, even lifetime); React quickly on re-exposure"]]},{"id":37107,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A child had a first dose of a vaccine last year. Which statement about a later exposure to the real pathogen is consistent with the lecture?","o":["The vaccine produced the primary response, so a later exposure triggers a faster, IgG-led secondary response","The vaccine does not count as antigen exposure, so the child mounts a slow primary response when the real pathogen arrives","The later response will be IgM-led and slower than before","The child will make only IgE against the pathogen","The child will have no antibody response because tolerance develops"],"e":"Antigen exposure can result from pathogen infection or vaccination. The first dose of a vaccine produces the primary response. Meeting the same antigen again leads to a secondary response that is faster, larger and primarily IgG-led, thanks to memory B cells.","w":["Vaccination counts as antigen exposure.","The secondary response is IgG-led and faster.","IgE is not the main secondary antibody.","Vaccination leads to memory, not tolerance."],"k":"Vaccination = antigen exposure → primary response → later secondary response.","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: isotype produced?","Primary vs secondary: isotype produced?","Primary: IgM early; Secondary: IgG"],["","Why can re-exposure at any time trigger a full secondary response?","Why can re-exposure at any time trigger a full secondary response?","Memory B cells persist long-term"]]},{"id":37108,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Which pairing of property and primary/secondary response value is correct?","o":["Lag period: 4–7 days primary; 1–3 days secondary","Lag period: 1–3 days primary; 4–7 days secondary","Time to peak: 3–5 days primary; 7–10 days secondary","Responding cell: memory B cell primary; naive B cell secondary","Isotype: IgG primary; IgM secondary"],"e":"The comparison table gives: responding B cell naive (primary) vs memory (secondary); lag 4–7 days vs 1–3 days; time of peak 7–10 days vs 3–5 days; magnitude varies by antigen vs generally 100–1000 times higher; isotype IgM early vs IgG predominates; antigens thymus dependent and independent vs thymus dependent; affinity lower vs higher.","w":["The lag periods are reversed.","The peak times are reversed.","The responding cells are reversed.","IgM predominates early in the primary response; IgG in the secondary."],"k":"Primary lag 4–7 d, peak 7–10 d; secondary lag 1–3 d, peak 3–5 d.","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: lag period?","Primary vs secondary: lag period?","Primary: 4 to 7 days; Secondary: 1 to 3 days"]]},{"id":37109,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"How much higher is the peak antibody response in the secondary response than the primary, according to the table?","o":["Generally 100–1000 times higher","About 2 times higher","About 10 times higher","It is lower than the primary response","Exactly the same"],"e":"The comparison table states that the magnitude of the peak antibody response varies with the antigen in the primary response, but is generally 100–1000 times higher in the secondary response. On the graph's logarithmic y-axis the secondary total peak is far above the primary.","w":["The difference is far greater than twofold.","The difference is generally 100–1000-fold.","The secondary response is larger.","The responses differ greatly."],"k":"Secondary peak: generally 100–1000× primary.","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: magnitude of peak antibody?","Primary vs secondary: magnitude of peak antibody?","Primary: varies with antigen; Secondary: 100 to 1,000 times higher"]]},{"id":37110,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"Which statement about the antigens and affinity in primary versus secondary responses is correct?","o":["Primary: thymus dependent and independent, lower affinity; secondary: thymus dependent, higher affinity","Primary: thymus dependent antigens only, with higher affinity; secondary: thymus independent antigens, with lower affinity","Both use only thymus-independent antigens with equal affinity","Primary: higher affinity; secondary: lower affinity","Affinity is not compared in the table"],"e":"The table lists antigens as thymus dependent and thymus independent in the primary response and thymus dependent in the secondary response, with antibody affinity lower in the primary and higher in the secondary. Higher secondary affinity reflects affinity maturation.","w":["This reverses both rows.","The table lists different antigen types and affinities.","Secondary affinity is higher.","Affinity is listed: lower then higher."],"k":"Secondary response: thymus-dependent antigens, higher affinity.","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: antigens?","Primary vs secondary: antigens?","Primary: thymus dependent and independent; Secondary: thymus dependent"],["","Primary vs secondary: antibody affinity?","Primary vs secondary: antibody affinity?","Primary: lower; Secondary: higher"]]},{"id":37111,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"On this graph, which feature explains why the secondary response peak looks so much higher than the primary?","o":["The y-axis is logarithmic and the secondary peak is many-fold higher","The y-axis is linear, and the two peaks are in fact of equal height","The primary peak is mostly IgG, the secondary mostly IgM","The secondary response has a longer lag","The graph shows IgE only"],"e":"The y-axis (antibody concentration in serum, units per ml) is logarithmic from 0.01 to 100, so each step is tenfold. The secondary total peak is far above the primary. In the primary response IgM leads and IgG appears late; in the secondary response IgM is small and IgG is large and fast.","w":["The axis is logarithmic and the peaks differ greatly.","IgM leads the primary response; IgG dominates the secondary.","The secondary response has a shorter lag.","The graph shows IgM, IgG and total antibody."],"k":"Log y-axis; secondary peak many-fold higher and IgG-dominated.","f":"bc25","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: magnitude of peak antibody?","Primary vs secondary: magnitude of peak antibody?","Primary: varies with antigen; Secondary: 100 to 1,000 times higher"]]},{"id":37112,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"In this graph, which label marks the small antibody peak seen during the secondary response?","o":["6","2","3","7","1"],"e":"The labels show: 1 the lag; 2 IgM leading the primary response; 3 IgG appearing late in the primary response; 4 the first antigen exposure; 5 the second antigen exposure; 6 a small secondary IgM peak; 7 the large, fast secondary IgG peak.","w":["2 is IgM in the primary response.","3 is IgG appearing late in the primary response.","7 is the large secondary IgG peak.","1 is the lag."],"k":"Secondary response: small IgM, large IgG.","f":"bc25","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: time of peak response?","Primary vs secondary: time of peak response?","Primary: 7 to 10 days; Secondary: 3 to 5 days"]]},{"id":37113,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"Which statement about the antigen-binding domain of antibodies is correct?","o":["It is not germline encoded; it is generated by somatic recombination and affinity maturation","It is copied unchanged from the germline DNA that every somatic cell carries","It is encoded by the constant region genes","It is made by T cells and given to B cells","It is fixed and cannot change after the first exposure"],"e":"The summary states that the antigen-binding domain is not germline encoded, but rather is generated by the genetic rearrangement processes of somatic recombination and affinity maturation. This gives a huge repertoire and allows binding to improve during infection.","w":["B cells rearrange their DNA, so it is not a germline copy.","The variable region, not the constant region, forms the binding site.","B cells make their own antibodies.","Affinity maturation changes it after first exposure."],"k":"Binding domain: generated by somatic recombination + affinity maturation.","rn":[["","Is the antigen binding domain germline encoded?","Is the antigen binding domain germline encoded?","No; Made by somatic recombination and affinity maturation"]]},{"id":37114,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"As the primary response matures into the secondary response, what stays the same and what changes?","o":["Specificity stays the same; effector functions change by swapping the Fc domain","Specificity changes to a new antigen while the Fc domain stays exactly the same","Both specificity and Fc domain stay the same","Both specificity and Fc domain change","The antibody stops binding antigen"],"e":"As the primary immune response matures into the secondary (more robust) response, antibodies to the invading pathogen maintain their specificity, but are able to adapt their effector functions by swapping the Fc domain: isotype switching.","w":["Specificity is kept; the Fc domain changes.","The Fc domain changes in isotype switching.","Specificity is maintained.","Antibodies keep binding the antigen."],"k":"Isotype switching: same specificity, new Fc effector function.","rn":[["","During the immune response, what do antibodies keep and what do they change?","During the immune response, what do antibodies keep and what do they change?","Keep specificity; Change effector function (Fc domain)"]]},{"id":37115,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","a":["isotype switching","class switching","class switch","isotype switch","class switch recombination"],"s":"What is the name for the change in antibody class, by swapping the constant (Fc) domain, while keeping the same antigen specificity?","e":"Isotype (class) switching changes the constant domain of the antibody, for example from IgM to IgG, so the antibody keeps its specificity but interacts with different effector molecules. It cannot go back from IgG to IgM.","k":"Isotype/class switching swaps the Fc domain, keeping specificity.","rn":[["","Class switching is also called?","Class switching is also called?","Isotype switching"],["","Class Switching","Class Switching","Changes the constant domain; Same antigen affinity, different effector molecules"]]},{"id":37116,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","a":["memory b cells","memory b cell","memory cells","memory cell"],"s":"Which cells, produced after the first exposure, determine the speed of the secondary response?","e":"Memory B cells produced after the first exposure quickly produce plasma cells on a second exposure, making the secondary response faster and largely IgG. They can persist for a lifetime.","k":"Memory B cells drive the fast secondary response.","rn":[["","What makes the secondary response fast?","What makes the secondary response fast?","Memory B cells; Quickly produce plasma cells"]]},{"id":37117,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","a":["plasma cells","plasma cell"],"s":"Name the short-lived cells that are factories for antibody production.","e":"Plasma cells are identical daughter clones of an antigen-binding B cell. They are factories for antibody production but live for a short period of time, unlike long-lived memory cells.","k":"Plasma cells: short-lived antibody factories.","rn":[["","Plasma Cells","Plasma Cells","Identical daughter clones; Antibody factories; Short lifespan"]]},{"id":37118,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A patient's B cells cannot class switch. Which pattern would you expect in a secondary response?","o":["Antibody stays IgM-dominated instead of switching to IgG","No antibody is produced at all","The response becomes IgE-dominated","The antibody loses its antigen specificity","Memory B cells cannot form at all"],"e":"Normally class switching changes the constant region so that IgG becomes the main antibody, and the secondary response is predominantly IgG. Without switching, the antibody would remain IgM. Specificity depends on the variable region, which is unaffected by class switching.","w":["Antibody can still be made; only the class is affected.","Without switching, IgE is not produced either.","Specificity comes from the variable region, which is unchanged.","Memory formation is not described as depending on class switching."],"k":"No class switching → IgM persists instead of IgG.","rn":[["","Is a switch back from IgG to IgM possible?","Is a switch back from IgG to IgM possible?","No"],["","What makes IgG appear in the primary response?","What makes IgG appear in the primary response?","Class switching; Affinity maturation"],["","Why do B cells switch to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","Why do B cells switch to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","IgM uses more energy; IgG more energy efficient"]]},{"id":37119,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"Which pairing of antibody class and function is NOT correct?","o":["IgD: crosses the placenta","IgA: protects mucosal surfaces","IgE: binds mast cells and basophils","IgM: interacts most with complement","IgG: binds macrophages most strongly"],"e":"From the properties table and speaker notes: IgG is most abundant, binds macrophages best and is the only class to cross the placenta; IgA protects mucosal surfaces; IgM interacts most with complement and is very efficient against bacteria; IgD is a lymphocyte-bound receptor on B cells; IgE binds mast cells and basophils.","w":["IgA does protect mucosal surfaces.","IgE does bind mast cells and basophils.","IgM does interact most with complement.","IgG does bind macrophages most strongly."],"k":"Only IgG crosses the placenta; IgD is on B cells.","rn":[["","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","IgG"],["","Which isotypes do NOT cross the placenta?","Which isotypes do NOT cross the placenta?","IgA; IgM; IgD; IgE"]]},{"id":37120,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"A newborn has protective IgG against measles in the first weeks of life, without ever being infected. What explains this?","o":["Maternal IgG crossed the placenta","The newborn made a secondary response at birth","Maternal IgM crossed the placenta","The newborn's B cells were exposed to measles in the thymus","IgA from the mother entered the fetal blood"],"e":"IgG is the only isotype that crosses the placenta (++ in the properties table). The lecturer linked this to IgG being smaller than IgA and IgM, so newborns gain immunity early in life. IgM and IgA do not cross the placenta.","w":["A secondary response needs a prior exposure.","IgM does not cross the placenta.","B cells do not develop in the thymus.","IgA does not cross the placenta."],"k":"Newborn early immunity comes from maternal IgG across the placenta.","rn":[["","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","IgG"]]},{"id":37121,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A 20-year-old medical student receives a first dose of a vaccine. Antibody levels are measured every few days: nothing is detectable for 5 days, a small peak occurs at day 9, and the early antibody is mostly a pentamer. Six months later she gets a booster. This time antibody rises within 2 days and peaks at day 4.<br><br>Compared with the first peak, what are the booster peak's isotype and size most likely to be?","o":["Mostly IgG, about 100–1000 times higher","Mostly IgM, about the same height as the first peak","Mostly IgM, about 100–1000 times higher than the first","Mostly IgE, about ten times lower than the first peak","Mostly IgD, roughly equal to the first peak"],"e":"Step 1: the first dose produced a primary response: lag 4–7 days, peak 7–10 days, IgM (a pentamer) predominating early. Step 2: the booster triggers a secondary response from memory B cells: lag 1–3 days, peak 3–5 days, IgG predominating, and a peak generally 100–1000 times higher than the primary, with higher affinity.","w":["IgM predominates early in the primary response; the secondary peak is much higher.","The secondary response is predominantly IgG, not IgM.","IgE is linked with allergy and mast cells, not the secondary response peak.","IgD is on B cell surfaces; the secondary response is IgG-dominated and far larger."],"k":"Secondary response: faster, IgG, 100–1000× higher.","rn":[["","Primary vs secondary: magnitude of peak antibody?","Primary vs secondary: magnitude of peak antibody?","Primary: varies with antigen; Secondary: 100 to 1,000 times higher"]]},{"id":37122,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"A newborn baby is found to have antibodies in her blood against an infection her mother had years ago, even though the baby has never been exposed to it. The paediatrician explains that only one class of maternal antibody could have reached the baby before birth.<br><br>Which antibody is this, and which feature of it explains the transfer?","o":["IgG; it is the only class that crosses the placenta, being smaller than IgA and IgM","IgM; its pentamer has ten binding sites, fixes complement best and is made first in the fetus","IgA; its secretory piece carries it across mucous membranes","IgE; it binds mast cells and basophils","IgD; it is bound on B cell surfaces"],"e":"Step 1: the properties table shows only IgG crosses the placenta (++); IgA, IgM, IgD and IgE do not. Step 2: the lecturer explained IgG is smaller than IgA and IgM, so newborns gain immunity early in life from maternal IgG.","w":["IgM does not cross the placenta, despite strong complement interaction.","sIgA protects mucosal surfaces; it does not cross the placenta.","IgE does not cross the placenta.","IgD is a lymphocyte-bound receptor and does not cross the placenta."],"k":"Only IgG crosses the placenta.","rn":[["","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","Which isotype crosses the placenta?","IgG"],["","Why can IgG cross the placenta?","Why can IgG cross the placenta?","It is small compared with IgA and IgM"],["","Which isotypes do NOT cross the placenta?","Which isotypes do NOT cross the placenta?","IgA; IgM; IgD; IgE"]]},{"id":37123,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"In a laboratory experiment, mature B cells are exposed to their matching antigen, which binds their B cell receptors, but no growth factor (such as interleukin-2) is added. A second dish receives both antigen and interleukin-2. Weeks later, the B cells from the first dish are given antigen and interleukin-2 together but still fail to respond.<br><br>What most likely happened to the B cells in the first dish?","o":["Anergy or apoptosis, because only one of two signals was given","Clonal expansion into plasma and memory cells, because antigen alone is enough","Class switching to IgE, because no growth factor was present","Negative selection in the spleen, because they reacted to self","Affinity maturation, which improved their binding but stopped division"],"e":"Step 1: clonal expansion needs two signals: antigen binding the receptor and growth factor stimulation (interleukin-2). Step 2: with only one signal the cell becomes anergic (cannot be activated later) or dies by apoptosis, which explains why the first-dish cells later fail to respond.","w":["Antigen alone is only one signal.","Class switching is not described as resulting from a missing growth factor.","Negative selection removes self-reactive B cells in the bone marrow.","Affinity maturation needs activation; it would not prevent later responses."],"k":"Two signals for activation; one alone → anergy or apoptosis.","rn":[["","What happens if only one activation signal is received?","What happens if only one activation signal is received?","Anergy; Or apoptosis"]]},{"id":37124,"t":"Affinity and Avidity","s":"Researchers compare three molecules binding the same viral surface: a single Fab fragment, a whole IgG and a pentameric IgM. The Fab binds within seconds but detaches within tens of minutes, letting virus escape. The IgG stays bound for hours to days. The IgM holds the antigen even though its individual binding sites have not yet matured.<br><br>Which concept best explains the longer binding of IgG and IgM?","o":["Avidity: many arms make simultaneous detachment unlikely","Affinity: each single arm of IgM binds more strongly than a Fab","Glycosylation: carbohydrates on the constant region hold antigen","Class switching: changing the constant region increases binding","Agglutination: clumps are trapped in mucus and cleared"],"e":"Step 1: a single Fab is univalent; affinity can be high but binding is reversible (on in seconds, off in tens of minutes). Step 2: with two or more arms each binds independently, so the chance all detach at once is low: this functional affinity is avidity, up to 10³-fold for IgG and 10⁷-fold for IgM. IgM's avidity compensates for affinity not yet fully developed.","w":["IgM's early affinity is not yet fully developed; avidity compensates.","Glycosylation rarely affects antigen binding.","Class switching changes effector partners, not antigen binding.","Agglutination is a function of antibodies, not the reason binding lasts."],"k":"Avidity = combined strength of many sites (IgG ×10³, IgM ×10⁷).","rn":[["","Why does an intact IgG stay bound longer than a Fab?","Why does an intact IgG stay bound longer than a Fab?","Both arms unlikely to detach at once"]]},{"id":37125,"t":"Somatic Recombination","s":"A child is born with a defect in the recombination activation enzymes RAG-1 and RAG-2. His B cell progenitors in the bone marrow are present, and his other somatic cells have normal DNA.<br><br>What is the most likely effect on his antibodies?","o":["Antibody gene segments cannot be spliced, so no diverse antibodies form","Antibodies form normally but cannot switch from IgM to IgG","Antibodies form but cannot leave the B cell, so they stay on the membrane as BCRs only","Antibodies form but lack a J chain, so IgM cannot polymerise","Antibodies form but cannot cross the placenta to the fetus"],"e":"Step 1: antibody chains are built by somatic (V(D)J) recombination, splicing V, D, J and C segments at the DNA level; this needs recombination signal sequences and recombination activation enzymes including RAG-1/2. Step 2: without RAG-1/2, segments cannot be joined, so B cells cannot build the diverse antibody repertoire (each B cell normally making one specific antibody).","w":["Class switching needs a formed antibody first; recombination itself fails.","The BCR is the first antibody made after recombination, which cannot occur.","J chains join IgM and IgA units; they are not RAG products.","Placental transfer depends on IgG, which cannot form without recombination."],"k":"RAG-1/2 needed for V(D)J recombination and antibody diversity.","rn":[["","Somatic Recombination","Somatic Recombination","Splicing V, D, J and C gene segments together; DNA-level rearrangement"],["","Which gene segments form the heavy chain?","Which gene segments form the heavy chain?","V, D, J and C"],["","Which gene segments form the light chain?","Which gene segments form the light chain?","V, J and C (no D)"]]},{"id":37126,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A 35-year-old has a parasitic worm (helminth) infection. Biopsy shows eosinophils clustered on the worm's surface, releasing their granule contents. The eosinophils bind antibody via Fc receptors specific for one class of antibody.<br><br>Which antibody function and isotype are involved?","o":["Degranulation, via IgE and FcεR","Opsonisation, via IgG and macrophage Fc receptors","ADCC, via NK cells inducing apoptosis","Neutralisation, via IgA blocking host binding","Complement fixation, via IgM and the membrane attack complex"],"e":"Step 1: the antibody functions slide shows eosinophils with Fc receptors (FcεR and IgE) attacking a parasite (helminth) by degranulation. Step 2: IgE's listed role is initiation of inflammation in parasitic infections (and allergy symptoms), and it binds via FcεR.","w":["Opsonisation uses macrophage Fc receptors for phagocytosis.","ADCC is NK cell killing of antibody-coated cells.","Neutralisation blocks viruses and toxins binding host cells.","Complement fixation forms the MAC; it does not involve eosinophil granules."],"k":"Eosinophils degranulate on helminths via IgE/FcεR.","rn":[["","How does degranulation attack helminths?","How does degranulation attack helminths?","Antibody IgE coats the helminth; Eosinophil FcεR (Fc epsilon receptor) binds IgE; Eosinophil degranulates"]]},{"id":37127,"t":"B Cell Development and Maturation","s":"A 32-year-old develops antibodies that bind her own tissues. Investigations suggest that B cells reacting to her own (endogenous) antigens were not removed during development and have been released into the circulation.<br><br>At which stage did the failure most likely occur, and what is the result?","o":["Negative selection in the bone marrow; autoimmune disease","Class switching in the spleen after antigen exposure; immunodeficiency","Affinity maturation in the thymus; allergy","Clonal expansion in the lymph nodes; anergy","Glycosylation in the Golgi; graft rejection"],"e":"Step 1: in the bone marrow, after V(D)J recombination, negative selection eliminates B cells whose antibodies react with endogenous (self) antigens; self-reactive B cells are destroyed or suppressed. Step 2: if this control is lost, antibodies to self are released, causing autoimmune disease.","w":["Class switching changes isotype and would not cause immunodeficiency here.","Affinity maturation does not happen in the thymus.","Clonal expansion failure does not release self-reactive cells.","Glycosylation is a post-translational modification unrelated to self-tolerance."],"k":"Failed negative selection → self antibodies → autoimmunity.","rn":[["","Negative Selection","Negative Selection","B cells reacting with self-antigens are eliminated; In the bone marrow"],["","Which 2 processes happen to B cells in the bone marrow?","Which 2 processes happen to B cells in the bone marrow?","Somatic (V(D)J) recombination; Negative selection"]]},{"id":37128,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A patient's B cell clone first produces IgM against a bacterial antigen. Later the same clone produces IgG that binds the identical epitope, and now works well with macrophages. When the antigen returns years later, the clone produces IgG again rather than IgM.<br><br>Which statement best explains these observations?","o":["Only the constant region changed; switching is irreversible and IgG is more efficient","The variable region changed during the response, so the antibody now binds a new antigen on macrophages","The clone switched back to IgM, which is energetically cheaper","Affinity maturation changed the constant region to bind macrophages","The light chain gained a D segment during the second exposure"],"e":"Step 1: class switching changes only the constant domain: the same antigen is bound, but with different effector partners (IgG binds macrophages best, +++). Step 2: there is no switching back from IgG to IgM, and IgM uses more energy, so switching to IgG is more efficient.","w":["Specificity stays the same after switching.","Switching is one-way, and IgM is more energetically expensive.","Affinity maturation changes the variable domain.","Light chains are made of V, J and C segments only."],"k":"Class switch: constant region only, irreversible, same specificity.","rn":[["","Class Switching","Class Switching","Changes the constant domain; Same antigen affinity, different effector molecules"],["","Why do B cells switch to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","Why do B cells switch to IgG rather than keep making IgM?","IgM uses more energy; IgG more energy efficient"]]},{"id":37129,"t":"Antibody Isotypes","s":"A 9-month-old infant with an inherited lack of the secretory component has repeated gut infections. Her IgA can form dimers joined by a J chain but cannot be carried properly into the gut lumen.<br><br>What protective function of IgA is most impaired?","o":["Protection of mucosal surfaces, as IgA cannot cross the epithelium and resist proteolysis","Placental transfer of maternal immunity before birth, which normally depends on the secretory piece of IgA","Activation of mast cells and basophils in allergy","Complement fixation with the membrane attack complex","Display of antibody as a receptor on B cells"],"e":"Step 1: secretory IgA (sIgA) is a dimer joined by a J chain plus a secretory piece. The secretory piece protects it from proteolysis and helps secretion across mucous membranes (e.g. gut, respiratory tract). Step 2: sIgA is carried across epithelium (poly-Ig receptor, secretory component) into the lumen to protect against antigens; without it, mucosal protection fails.","w":["Placental transfer is IgG's role.","Mast cell binding is IgE's role.","IgA does not interact with complement (−).","Display on B cells is IgD's role."],"k":"Secretory piece: protects sIgA, carries it across mucosa.","rn":[["","Where is IgA found?","Where is IgA found?","Secretory tissues / mucosal surfaces; e.g. gut, respiratory tract"],["","IgA: key characteristic?","IgA: key characteristic?","External (mucosal) surface protection"],["","Which receptor carries IgA across epithelium?","Which receptor carries IgA across epithelium?","Poly-Ig receptor"]]},{"id":37130,"t":"Antibodies in the Immune Response","s":"A 60-year-old was infected with a virus 30 years ago. Today his blood antibody to that virus is low, but when he is re-exposed he mounts a rapid, large antibody response with no illness. A biopsy shows long-lived B cells in his tissues that carry antibody on their membrane.<br><br>Which cells explain the rapid response, and what do they produce?","o":["Memory B cells, which quickly make plasma cells that secrete antibody","Plasma cells, which stayed as antibody factories for 30 years","Naive B cells, which first produce IgD and IgM","NK cells, which kill antibody-coated cells by ADCC","Immature B cells, which circulate briefly looking for antigen"],"e":"Step 1: memory cells are very long-lived (can stay in tissues for decades), carry antibody on their membrane and react fast to a repeat infection. Step 2: on re-exposure they quickly make plasma cells (short-lived antibody factories), giving a secondary response that is faster and larger, even when circulating IgG has fallen.","w":["Plasma cells are short-lived.","Naive B cells drive the primary response.","NK cells are not antibody-producing cells.","Immature B cells are not meant to last long."],"k":"Long-lived memory B cells → rapid plasma cell response.","rn":[["","What makes the secondary response fast?","What makes the secondary response fast?","Memory B cells; Quickly produce plasma cells"]]}]);
